#{[{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162855} :activity/id #uuid "e16c3950-01f1-5af7-ae8f-999ba2fcc9fa" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162855 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163230 :tutorial/content "In certain applications involving quadratic forms, an important question is whether a particular quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf x)$ is *always* positive or *always* negative.\n\nWe classify quadratic forms using the following terminology:\n\n- A quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is **positive-definite** if $Q(\\mathbf{x}) > 0$ for all $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n \\setminus \\{ \\mathbf{0} \\}.$\n\nFor example, the quadratic form\n\n$$\nS(\\mathbf{x})=8x_1^2+3x_2^2\n$$\n\nis positive-definite since\n$8x_1^2+3x_2^2 > 0$\nfor all nonzero $\\mathbf{x}.$\n\n![](images/52/52e43ebb6fa34f40a73358f88aed62846332126c4fe080072c211945f54b412a.png)\n\n- A quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is **negative-definite** if $Q(\\mathbf{x}) <     0$ for all $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n \\setminus \\{ \\mathbf{0} \\}.$\n\nFor example, the quadratic form \n\n$$\nT(\\mathbf{x})=-x_1^2-3x_2^2\n$$\n\nis negative-definite since\n$-x_1^2-3x_2^2 \\lt 0$\nfor all nonzero $\\mathbf{x}.$\n\n![](images/a8/a86f80966c04f960823db91bfc8711eef2bf22a81502e69b5048afc5b4799cb2.png)\n\n- A quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is **indefinite** if \n\n- $Q(\\mathbf{x}_1) > 0$ for some $\\mathbf{x}_1 \\in \\mathbb{R}^n,$ and\n\n- $Q(\\mathbf{x}_2) <     0$ for some $\\mathbf{x}_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}^n.$\n\nFor example, the form\n\n$$\nR(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2+2x_2^2-6x_1x_2\n$$\n\nis indefinite. Substituting ![](images/09/094a61c791563994cb520b40179d77546f776bfb9148851e211ec8ed326c279a.svg) we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R({x}_{1}) & ={1}^{2}+2\\cdot {0}^{2}-6\\cdot 1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =1+0-0 \\\\  & =1>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting ![](images/f4/f4995691f0b24159baeb3cbb1ee51a9f44c72958c9288809f28f387d17b2810d.svg) we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R({x}_{2}) & ={1}^{2}+2\\cdot {1}^{2}-6\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1+2-6 \\\\  & =-3<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c6542a88-fba4-557b-ae08-48d95747b079" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4889 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6be8e550-b338-5637-a624-ef1b87eabd9b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162895}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162895 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162008 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6289ad34-2393-5f5f-b7f0-a1ab1a3916f4"} :step/id #uuid "ca8c0245-0936-52e2-ab35-4e10d9592791" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163054}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162896 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163241 :tutorial/content "Given a quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf x) = \\mathbf{x}^T A \\mathbf{x}$ with a symmetric matrix $A$,  we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive,\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nUsing the same criteria, we can also classify the symmetric matrix $A$ as positive-definite, positive semi-definite, etc.\n\nFor example, consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/85/8582c8b840b1e4696b9f83cb2c117d67e3b832e40b1ded716e0dcd23752c6f9c.svg)\n\nComputing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-5-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ -1 & -5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-5-\\lambda )(-5-\\lambda )-1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+10\\lambda +24 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)(\\lambda +6) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-4,\\,-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative, the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf x) =  \\mathbf{x}^T A \\, \\mathbf{x}$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is negative-definite." :tutorial/id #uuid "c4b47113-5633-5c1b-bb17-e6ec70b5a550" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4952 :tutorial/title "Using Eigenvalues to Classify Quadratic Forms"} :step/id #uuid "2d8db3b7-e619-5958-9482-0acdf61cd3e6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162897}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162897 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162009 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4105f1cb-eb48-5946-92f7-cd5e67689912"} :step/id #uuid "20343d54-d701-5e59-9cab-48adc9d25870" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163055}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162898 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163242 :tutorial/content "Let ![](images/c6/c6f4a56eed9073b3541e4398db9fc99f2e8ca94f651762e1f650bfe54865ca49.svg) for ![](images/16/1621ad9f5426c8fe19e2d5a00c5f92a60420aeaed7bfe623db70c9ab4e57527d.svg)  denote the leading principal minors of an ![](images/b0/b0cdde792f80dc6b0c5defb2f8d767ae115ecf922fa46f2a233f10959e509870.svg) matrix ![](images/c8/c8c72090d28b539bed2177052b06540383d63ac720008f1b301dafcac4fa6227.svg) \n\nAccording to **Sylvester's criterion**, the quadratic form ![](images/40/40138f7d0f5e38d137af53df63df0473cc827a5d8c8f957c7bd62d0ac2a30694.svg) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ![](images/54/54ac4b9f53d36b439544b913f7ba0dcfb153aa76b9b9624632a41cd9392ce5ac.svg) for all ![](images/af/af25353ac60910a8cca7edfd3ea5312dea05646d41cd68c1cc45156c06391298.svg)\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all ![](images/af/af25353ac60910a8cca7edfd3ea5312dea05646d41cd68c1cc45156c06391298.svg) we have ![](images/03/039aff55ff839029e398761cdb94f09b8c2203413637d6f41ca9c680659cc8d2.svg) when ![](images/33/33f73a318016d781b6ae1aa913c2f1807ab5c7e6c2901e508fc56561fbe26b45.svg) is odd, and ![](images/54/54ac4b9f53d36b439544b913f7ba0dcfb153aa76b9b9624632a41cd9392ce5ac.svg) when ![](images/33/33f73a318016d781b6ae1aa913c2f1807ab5c7e6c2901e508fc56561fbe26b45.svg) is even.\n\nIn particular, if ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) is negative-definite, then the leading principal minors alternate in sign, with the *first* leading principal minors being negative.\n\nFor example, consider the matrix \n\n![](images/bc/bc72eb8a57a1af3a4cc1526c071f7611131359d87b1ed56417e626aad662c9cb.svg)\n\nLet's compute its leading leading principal minors:\n\n![](images/84/844bf128565ccaeee211fcbeca2179b870901879303ce3e911956d65b468926f.svg)\n\nAll leading principal minors are positive. Therefore, by Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ![](images/40/40138f7d0f5e38d137af53df63df0473cc827a5d8c8f957c7bd62d0ac2a30694.svg) is positive-definite." :tutorial/id #uuid "721cf6ec-0dbe-515f-ae9f-63c855607e1e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4953 :tutorial/title "Sylvester's Criterion"} :step/id #uuid "5cc719a5-ab07-5159-9a43-3394f7193bc5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162899}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162899 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162010 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbebdd1b-0716-55cb-8df6-12a35b517ab5"} :step/id #uuid "d2fe4efb-5814-5b5d-a57c-b5cb76832908"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163054 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163318 :tutorial/content "There are two further classifications of quadratic forms that turn out to be useful:\n\n- A quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is **positive semi-definite** if $Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\geq 0$ for all $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n.$\n\nFor example, the form $A(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2$ over $\\mathbb{R}^2$ is positive semi-definite. Notice that this particular form is not positive-definite since it gives zero on the nonzero vector ![](images/d3/d32277694c7bd75cf76ea413fba14b25a679f84e8194c07af201e103d23a1056.svg) \n\n![](images/8d/8d0adf62e90acc2f5a2a3fdbad2b87fc53e4f50145e38d8120cd932282afbb42.png)\n\n- A quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is **negative semi-definite** if $Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\leq 0$ for all $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n.$ \n\nFor example, the form $B(\\mathbf{x})=-x_1^2$ over $\\mathbb{R}^2$ is negative semi-definite. Notice that this particular form is not negative-definite since it gives zero on the nonzero vector ![](images/05/0592bc60943ac605d21672288a437282e3dddd52384b518f8869645f05540662.svg) \n\n![](images/76/76feacaad709f2f3a3c1dac8c0ee88aa461aed827270d0127f61de38d95489b6.png)\n\nIt's also worth noting that\n\n- any positive-definite quadratic form is positive semi-definite, and\n\n- any negative-definite quadratic form is negative semi-definite." :tutorial/id #uuid "1f10e579-6a9a-5929-95df-033a67f11e7b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6137 :tutorial/title "Positive and Negative Semi-Definite Quadratic Forms"} :step/id #uuid "2b84589b-e820-5864-af80-1c00cdcc8be5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162896}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163055 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163319 :tutorial/content "The **leading principal minors** of an ![](images/b0/b0cdde792f80dc6b0c5defb2f8d767ae115ecf922fa46f2a233f10959e509870.svg) matrix are the ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) determinants formed by the first ![](images/3a/3a4ef1c1b261a53f9c45d0c90f2d44b155125fe1e7b1359406830111b4846f2f.svg) rows and first ![](images/3a/3a4ef1c1b261a53f9c45d0c90f2d44b155125fe1e7b1359406830111b4846f2f.svg) columns of ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) for ![](images/02/025dfd41d08c5cd679c6635316e6d92c7b612c1db8a0d5c88ca3633eb5dc3d0d.svg) The leading principal minors of ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) are denoted ![](images/d4/d4d4f854b6186e5a619b024a48f4017756b1de3fcf8f3c90d80e5a251bc3de93.svg)\n\nNote that:\n\n- the first leading principal minors ![](images/e9/e983ef296ac0b20ed0beb7771d7616ab810886d775bd8e343f03f78c757ee678.svg) equals ![](images/1c/1caa82c7c23331cc78782a151b62bafd5f6d9acee12c7ae29dc74556ebc16c1c.svg) and\n\n- the ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg)th leading principal minors ![](images/a2/a27bb42bcd37b2e4ba1416cff413d166b960a686a26ca5085da9be8661d8faf8.svg) equals ![](images/3a/3a950a6005df11dc3df83b40a796c8e5ce962b591e040207bb961da532ec92dd.svg)\n\nFor example, consider the matrix ![](images/c2/c2c85081d1f89d74b8ebb2761496d211d7052193809b7864e7983437f5d0b80f.svg) given by\n\n![](images/26/261bd51313b7630f2c14550d0318e1094a7915316c165e8555ddd77148b8b26d.svg)\n\nSince ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) is a ![](images/59/599d5897394ff1110f6a3bac7053c6836d231de17effad76a1c633d1aed81b33.svg) matrix, it has ![](images/6c/6cb44f25e489afb06ec62d2e95c4d235ece77eb36e2327b856264d2fb8b22c21.svg) leading principal minors. Let's compute them:\n\n- The first leading principal minors is the determinant formed by the first row and first column of ![](images/18/18e24ea087f70c1cc4a9689aa1571074db08e3e830283838c983a5c4fb976602.svg) \n![](images/9a/9ab81179136bdf16c09d64def927c7175884692ecd6f359143bb7333e91b3a2b.svg)\n\n- The second leading principal minors is the determinant formed by the first ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) rows and first ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) columns of ![](images/18/18e24ea087f70c1cc4a9689aa1571074db08e3e830283838c983a5c4fb976602.svg) \n![](images/03/032d2edce1d92da3a0b49aa2e582f61896280571c9fd2c1cc277b7cc77af353e.svg)\n\n- The third leading principal minors is the determinant formed by the first ![](images/6c/6cb44f25e489afb06ec62d2e95c4d235ece77eb36e2327b856264d2fb8b22c21.svg) rows and first ![](images/6c/6cb44f25e489afb06ec62d2e95c4d235ece77eb36e2327b856264d2fb8b22c21.svg) columns of ![](images/18/18e24ea087f70c1cc4a9689aa1571074db08e3e830283838c983a5c4fb976602.svg)\n\n![](images/9d/9d1f53a40f9e6dfcb44f231287b67b70a42d1cd10dbf99c803517f21af7265eb.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "8136146f-4191-5b4d-be2d-f33bf2b8d1f6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6138 :tutorial/title "The Leading Principal Minors of a Square Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "ecf1e76d-67dd-5f72-a6ec-7fba2fc8aff6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162898}}] :activity/title "Positive-Definite and Negative-Definite Quadratic Forms" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162222} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504717} :activity/id #uuid "fb6cf230-95ba-52e0-b584-587b6c68c3e0" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504711 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504680 :question/id #uuid "c8c9dba6-d928-5ac1-ba3e-1db8d33cfb50"} :step/id #uuid "10c29fe9-08fc-5279-949b-8249dea25825" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504719}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504717 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360498 :question/id #uuid "1a997acb-de91-5ebd-a6cc-103226757603"} :step/id #uuid "380d136a-e07a-515f-bfdc-c12a82904a6e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504711}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504718 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269221 :question/id #uuid "30844de8-e636-5e8f-ba19-823c3f8b1cf5"} :step/id #uuid "3b3b3471-3890-502c-9e74-ac7e9eadbeba"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504719 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360502 :question/id #uuid "43115d1d-8694-5a95-b413-4600fbd2bc68"} :step/id #uuid "3edbcb74-ef0e-5d2b-b4a5-9224616cec9d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504735}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504735 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360503 :question/id #uuid "7d5f731a-8eca-5400-88d7-dd10fc541120"} :step/id #uuid "c73b7c66-857e-5a2c-9637-ba3dda90e9f0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504718}}] :activity/title "Positive-Definite and Negative-Definite Quadratic Forms" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504030} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525118} :activity/id #uuid "5dca4ae1-f844-539e-b380-ba8dbc5f5355" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525067 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524999 :question/id #uuid "7aa930b4-3c77-5008-aaf5-9bbf298703c7"} :step/id #uuid "014ca587-44a7-5c4b-a2b6-7b58d26e96d2"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525071 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525025 :question/id #uuid "aa5d7a51-2b25-530e-bc34-f2b29d7db7d9"} :step/id #uuid "06a5f906-8b72-514b-9062-d1bf33299610" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525097}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525094 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162638 :question/id #uuid "9a27d2cf-0561-5715-9600-290d8804741e"} :step/id #uuid "561ae071-c31b-5dd5-8e25-204838ce97f8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525071}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525097 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525014 :question/id #uuid "95072baf-8f6f-5ac9-b278-bac347e84d65"} :step/id #uuid "5937adbb-f9b5-52b1-9c19-002f11b099e9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525067}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525118 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162636 :question/id #uuid "8a87a7ac-21af-54b9-b7c7-9dfc14e551b6"} :step/id #uuid "a02e4cde-95d3-5443-90f2-5b20307605ab" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525094}}] :activity/title "Positive-Definite and Negative-Definite Quadratic Forms" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524367}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186048555 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162008 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161800 :question/id #uuid "f3f6f5b1-bc25-586c-9d4d-dfeb64cfa077" :question/math-academy-id "e-8262" :question/problem "Consider the vector ![](images/e1/e1af4903eb9348ae20fc0de7ecdcbd5837257a85ae6ebd622d359c82b8719f85.svg) By evaluating the following quadratic forms at $\\mathbf{x}$ **only,** determine which of them **cannot** be negative-definite.\n\nI. $R(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2 - 3x_2^2 + 5x_1x_2$\n\nII. ![](images/4d/4d69526b89a09769a7fff19f231d31f9d6b1ac641916320fdb7483f8f2ffa9e2.svg)\n\nIII. $T(\\mathbf{x})=x_2^2 + 8x_1x_2$" :question/worked-solution "A quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is negative-definite if $Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\lt 0$ for all $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n \\setminus \\{ \\mathbf{0} \\}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms by substituting ![](images/06/067337418c7a579839e3ec13f52df9133d2db1a8cba58971dd25704087b707a7.svg)\n\n- Quadratic form I *might* be negative-definite. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & ={2}^{2}-3\\cdot {7}^{2}+5\\cdot 2\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =4-147+70 \\\\  & =-73<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form II *cannot* be negative-definite since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 14\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =110>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form III *cannot* be negative-definite since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(x) & ={7}^{2}+8\\cdot 2\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =49+112 \\\\  & =161>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6289ad34-2393-5f5f-b7f0-a1ab1a3916f4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162634 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16b5e14d-829f-5937-ba9c-fd1598dfd032" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460401} {:answer/id #uuid "2b15cd94-9900-5ec3-b324-7c8de72f8378" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460402}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460402} :answer-field/id #uuid "7eef49da-ab56-5f63-9232-a5c3209069b9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461067} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd50e9e3-7d6e-5a99-bbb3-83c0411a955a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460403} {:answer/id #uuid "fdec5c33-ce90-5d1f-b401-3adcbfe452b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460403} :answer-field/id #uuid "c054462a-1b17-563a-9bca-0de76f27561c" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461068} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c9182d9-8713-5210-af2a-b1ddcd840e25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460405} {:answer/id #uuid "0523bbf6-1585-5852-a37a-544ef336c339" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460405} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b4d2033-06b6-5ccf-b5f8-aab0b12b6d1b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461069}] :question/id #uuid "19082f66-1046-53f5-8de5-47b2fc5eafb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-94462" :question/problem "$$\n\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$ By evaluating the following quadratic forms at $x$ **only,** determine which of them **cannot** be positive-definite.\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $R(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+5{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$\n\n- **II.** $S(x)={x}^{T}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 9\\end{bmatrix}x$\n\n- **III.** $T(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$" :question/worked-solution "A quadratic form $Q(x)$ is positive-definite if $Q(x)>0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms by substituting $x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}:$\n\n- Quadratic form I *cannot* be positive-definite since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & ={1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+5\\cdot 1\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =1+4-10 \\\\  & =-5<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form II *might* be positive-definite. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -20\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =45>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form III *might* be positive-definite. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(x) & ={1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+1\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =1+4-2 \\\\  & =3>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162635 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "870b671c-2ff5-507a-b007-023effb212d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161103} {:answer/id #uuid "bc02a284-87f1-5e46-aec2-e2778916e673" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161104} {:answer/id #uuid "bfba3a47-4545-531c-b628-672de13984d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161105} {:answer/id #uuid "dd7f0c02-e60d-5472-b9f3-0e882e2a83e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161106} {:answer/id #uuid "094a6e53-ca8b-57c7-a3a6-fb021b94b809" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161107}] :answer-field/id #uuid "26cb2ebd-78e8-570c-a0ca-8ae36fbbfaa1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161102}] :question/id #uuid "4e931f8d-762f-5ebc-8f0a-25856d1ca578" :question/math-academy-id "q-94463" :question/problem "Which of the following statements regarding quadratic forms are true?\n\n1. $R(x) = x_{1}^{2} + 5x_{2}^{2}$ is positive-definite\n2. $S(x) = x^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}x$ is indefinite\n3. $T(x) =-3x_{1}x_{2}$ is negative-definite"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269218 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6eb6c871-e00b-55bb-be24-d71ea25f7553" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449514} {:answer/id #uuid "cf2f5c73-05c0-5880-a787-23f2d383e69c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449515}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449514} :answer-field/id #uuid "14e1058a-db49-54d3-b583-66942ce53dde" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450711} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ab20bbc-df2d-5dc9-a5a5-982386f45345" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449516} {:answer/id #uuid "1e8f1f92-2b9d-5f7f-bc2d-24f6eb154d57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449517}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449517} :answer-field/id #uuid "50f32587-2de7-571b-be31-977fd7b99f4b" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450712} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6f4dc65-45d4-5b00-9ce2-8f695a7fd648" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449518} {:answer/id #uuid "dd8caa95-465f-5b75-ae0e-1069da94d070" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449519}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449518} :answer-field/id #uuid "7cfb8ffa-e4df-5320-b046-1846fb4aeab9" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450713}] :question/id #uuid "44381bf9-c027-58df-ba36-2d3bed72d50c" :question/math-academy-id "q-139436" :question/problem "Which of the following statements regarding quadratic forms are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $R(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}$ is indefinite\n- **II.** $S(x)={x}^{T}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}x$ is negative-definite\n- **III.** $T(x)={x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ is positive-definite" :question/worked-solution "Recall that, a quadratic form $Q(x)$ is:\n\n- positive-definite if $Q(x)>0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0};$\n\n- negative-definite if $Q(x)<0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0};$\n\n- indefinite if \n\n- $Q({x}_{1})>0$ for some ${x}_{1}\\in {R}^{n},$ and\n\n- $Q({x}_{2})<0$ for some ${x}_{2}\\in {R}^{n}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0}.$ So, the quadratic form is positive-definite.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0},$ the quadratic form is negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is false. Substituting ${x}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({x}_{1}) & =1\\cdot 1=1>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting ${x}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({x}_{2}) & =1\\cdot (-1)=-1<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic form is indefinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360479 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "491282d8-087a-5b6a-872d-6b0a47d66b84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469722} {:answer/id #uuid "06e9071d-b647-5c18-bb45-e68fcc570e3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469723}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469722} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef7d7514-e7a3-5427-b315-a9a69bce3495" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470473} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83d2a2f4-ca6f-5c88-a824-d7acfdea58ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469724} {:answer/id #uuid "95ece7a6-9e69-5e78-8957-46a3c4175115" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469725}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469725} :answer-field/id #uuid "64c56b6b-2880-560b-9538-f33060b8721c" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470474} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5da7956-0eb9-5adb-bdb6-5c327d8b35b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469726} {:answer/id #uuid "91c1f39d-20a8-532f-b2ef-a41f48e42975" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469727}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469727} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4dd36d1-3aac-54c6-b8e2-1006ae8abc43" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470475}] :question/id #uuid "96ce050e-6292-5b2a-b7c7-0c1f7f51192d" :question/math-academy-id "q-94731" :question/problem "$$\n\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $x=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ By evaluating the following quadratic forms at $x$ **only,** determine which of them **cannot** be negative-definite.\n- **I.** $R(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}$\n- **II.** $S(x)={x}^{T}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x$\n- **III.** $T(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A quadratic form $Q(x)$ is negative-definite if $Q(x)<0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms by substituting $x=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}:$\n\n- Quadratic form I *might* be negative-definite. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & =(-1)^{2}-{3}^{2} \\\\  & =1-9 \\\\  & =-8<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form II *cannot* be negative-definite since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =33>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form III *cannot* be negative-definite since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(x) & =(-1)^{2}+3(3)^{2} \\\\  & =1+27 \\\\  & =28>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360480 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "195d16b7-57d2-5a98-ad60-1a8888bb7b27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469728} {:answer/id #uuid "4588d0a9-3d54-5976-bc71-51d75a7695f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469729}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469728} :answer-field/id #uuid "37ef1083-cbd3-5fa2-b49e-f0aa3bd44d3f" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470476} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dba4d436-16f6-5071-b037-fe3017f476b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469730} {:answer/id #uuid "5afbfc12-a6d2-5021-8f8e-5141947c51a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469731}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469731} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ad01140-9faf-5249-96a3-7f505c7b7804" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470477} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7f022e9-09a5-5650-8334-9f2aff11f4c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469732} {:answer/id #uuid "b36fdf26-1340-5fc8-be86-56dcbc4ad2c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469733}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469733} :answer-field/id #uuid "4676be30-c32d-51b3-b92b-dc162406a163" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470478}] :question/id #uuid "8c28bdf3-28d7-5fd9-bd3d-116dde5b3b96" :question/math-academy-id "q-94734" :question/problem "$$\n\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $x=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.$ By evaluating the following quadratic forms at $x$ **only,** determine which of them **cannot** be positive-definite.\n- **I.** $R(x)={x}_{2}^{2}+7{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$\n- **II.** $S(x)={x}^{T}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -9 \\\\ -9 & -2\\end{bmatrix}x$\n- **III.** $T(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}-3{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A quadratic form $Q(x)$ is positive-definite if $Q(x)>0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms by substituting $x=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}:$\n\n- Quadratic form I *might* be positive-definite. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & =(-6)^{2}+7\\cdot 0\\cdot (-6) \\\\  & =36+0 \\\\  & =36>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form II *cannot* be positive-definite since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -9 \\\\ -9 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}54 & 12\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-72<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form III *cannot* be positive-definite since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(x) & ={0}^{2}-(-6)^{2}-3\\cdot 0\\cdot (-6) \\\\  & =-36-0 \\\\  & =-36<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360482 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ed9f53d-8f5a-5d51-9fe5-b5701dfd17d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452059} {:answer/id #uuid "ba149761-6f03-59d5-b0a6-561327764bfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452060}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452059} :answer-field/id #uuid "943f3044-0061-5817-a4df-8dc03a7939e6" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452877} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d04386a-bf21-5e45-9825-7bc717ea9952" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452061} {:answer/id #uuid "b350b539-18ee-52b0-bf06-3473cf1e8fa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452062}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452062} :answer-field/id #uuid "1504f3a0-fa56-5d2a-8097-b25f32f379f3" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452878} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6da48a5-41d7-502e-b0c3-5047cccb111f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452063} {:answer/id #uuid "a640cd11-4a14-590a-9fa1-6e2e6e1f2e6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452064}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452063} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fc57d82-6dd3-56dd-bc98-4625624ce330" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452879}] :question/id #uuid "08e6755d-cc77-5492-aef8-539543b12cc4" :question/math-academy-id "q-94744" :question/problem "Which of the following statements regarding quadratic forms are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $R(x)=8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ is positive-definite\n- **II.** $S(x)={x}^{T}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}x$ is negative-definite\n- **III.** $T(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}$ is indefinite" :question/worked-solution "Recall that, a quadratic form $Q(x)$ is:\n\n- positive-definite if $Q(x)>0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0};$\n\n- negative-definite if $Q(x)<0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0};$\n\n- indefinite if \n\n- $Q({x}_{1})>0$ for some ${x}_{1}\\in {R}^{n},$ and\n\n- $Q({x}_{2})<0$ for some ${x}_{2}\\in {R}^{n}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Substituting ${x}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R({x}_{1}) & =8\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =8>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting ${x}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R({x}_{2}) & =8\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-8<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic form is indefinite.\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-2{x}_{1}^{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =-(2{x}_{1}^{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2})<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0}.$ So, the quadratic form is negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0},$ the quadratic form is positive-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491992 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17aaf2bf-6866-539d-93a7-3550c0ad750f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491501} {:answer/id #uuid "227651db-a977-5dd3-ae63-31b82448e8a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491524} {:answer/id #uuid "39d53e17-12df-5a79-a43a-0fed2ac2add7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491565} {:answer/id #uuid "a60c2556-0cdb-5e29-af28-82134e4a5d56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491744} {:answer/id #uuid "fd10519f-f7e9-5b30-b4d8-f93dee73dedf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491881}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491881} :answer-field/id #uuid "c928e1ea-bf78-5685-9e93-b81de2d2b29a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491962}] :question/id #uuid "128edce0-e99a-5468-9781-896334433102" :question/math-academy-id "q-94738" :question/problem "Which of the following statements regarding quadratic forms are true?\n\nI. $R(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2}$ is positive-definite\n\nII. $S(x)={x}^{T}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x$ is indefinite\n\nIII. $T(x)=-{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}$ is negative-definite" :question/worked-solution "Recall that, a quadratic form $Q(x)$ is:\n\n- positive-definite if $Q(x)>0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖\\{0\\};$\n\n- negative-definite if $Q(x)<0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖\\{0\\};$\n\n- indefinite if \n\n- $Q({x}_{1})>0$ for some ${x}_{1}\\in {R}^{n},$ and\n\n- $Q({x}_{2})<0$ for some ${x}_{2}\\in {R}^{n}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖\\{0\\},$ the quadratic form is positive-definite.\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting ${x}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S({x}_{1}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting ${x}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S({x}_{2}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-4<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic form is indefinite.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(x)=-{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖\\{0\\},$ the quadratic form is negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492023 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02810035-17a9-55fc-b380-765c670dc40f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491453} {:answer/id #uuid "14cd6899-3f95-5f26-a3c5-aa0416bc50e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491496} {:answer/id #uuid "9a4162ef-cda4-5332-8b18-a98ccf267ae6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491721} {:answer/id #uuid "b4965b24-eac7-5b69-b25a-ab93765a04a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491766} {:answer/id #uuid "e8449ed3-513e-567c-9648-42a7817f3366" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491852}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491496} :answer-field/id #uuid "50dc8df8-d5de-51dc-bac1-8423f21bcd96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491914}] :question/id #uuid "6c5d19b4-6f2c-5705-8395-4e5ce1c9092d" :question/math-academy-id "q-139434" :question/problem "$$\n\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.$ By evaluating the following quadratic forms at $x$ **only,** determine which of them **cannot** be positive-definite.\n\nI. $R(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$\n\nII. $S(x)={x}^{T}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x$\n\nIII. $T(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$" :question/worked-solution "A quadratic form $Q(x)$ is positive-definite if $Q(x)>0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖\\{0\\}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms by substituting $x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}:$\n\n- Quadratic form I *might* be positive-definite. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & ={1}^{2}+(-3)^{2}-2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =1+9+6 \\\\  & =16>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form II *might* be positive-definite. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =16>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form III *cannot* be positive-definite since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(x) & ={1}^{2}-(-3)^{2}+1\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =1-9-3 \\\\  & =-11<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492064 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "091a5b38-2aa6-55f9-b874-0fa3636ddb43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491473} {:answer/id #uuid "bbd92ac7-c403-5bac-b09a-0703a1e9d138" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491777} {:answer/id #uuid "be1a4089-0eb6-5431-b8df-be204224d848" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491781} {:answer/id #uuid "cea213d6-182d-5702-b5cc-bec35ad8997b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491805} {:answer/id #uuid "eb0278f8-5f06-5e83-aa79-9f9d6eefb54e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491473} :answer-field/id #uuid "eef1360f-bfc8-557c-ac1f-fead556c6f63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491978}] :question/id #uuid "ddb328b6-849a-5813-b5a8-23aaa33d5ab6" :question/math-academy-id "q-94733" :question/problem "$$\n\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $x=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ By evaluating the following quadratic forms at $x$ **only,** determine which of them **cannot** be negative-definite.\n\nI. $R(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}$\n\nII. $S(x)={x}^{T}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}x$\n\nIII. $T(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$" :question/worked-solution "A quadratic form $Q(x)$ is negative-definite if $Q(x)<0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖\\{0\\}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms by substituting $x=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}:$\n\n- Quadratic form I *cannot* be negative-definite since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & ={5}^{2}+2(0)^{2} \\\\  & =25+0 \\\\  & =25>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form II *might* be negative-definite. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-50<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form III *cannot* be negative-definite since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(x) & ={5}^{2}-{0}^{2}+2\\cdot 5\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =25+0 \\\\  & =25>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Quadratic Form Types Using Substitution"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162009 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161801 :question/id #uuid "178e4bbc-718c-5b9e-843a-91914ef16f64" :question/math-academy-id "e-8263" :question/problem "Consider the matrix ![](images/a6/a62ae39acd456a8b9fff6f42428f250ef569fd75849b77b9b69469f62c342afb.svg) Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\lambda_1=-1$ and $\\lambda_2=9$\n\nII. $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x}$ is indefinite\n\nIII. $A$ is positive-definite" :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf x) = \\mathbf{x}^T A \\mathbf{x},$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-4-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ 5 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-4-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )-25 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda -9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +9) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =1,\\,-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Since $A$ has positive and negative eigenvalues, the quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x}$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is indefinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4105f1cb-eb48-5946-92f7-cd5e67689912" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162636 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82db51ea-bf33-5d31-8dfc-545569cf5119" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161109} {:answer/id #uuid "8ba92099-726a-501d-80b0-a3110caa2f90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161110} {:answer/id #uuid "b2141f45-3669-53cb-993e-e53d7e5ab19e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161111} {:answer/id #uuid "063200f7-853c-54c9-90be-4e08e7fffe29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161112} {:answer/id #uuid "7e65e212-da6b-5bf3-be79-a15c98aee59d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161113}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161113} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5cef2fe-6358-5dbf-81eb-a5f9c43d0635" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161108}] :question/id #uuid "8a87a7ac-21af-54b9-b7c7-9dfc14e551b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-94464" :question/problem "Which of the following quadratic forms over ${R}^{2}$ are negative semi-definite?\n\nI. ${x}^{T}\\,Qx,$ where the eigenvalues of $Q$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5$\n\nII. ${x}^{T}\\,Rx,$ where the eigenvalues of $R$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-3$\n\nIII. ${x}^{T}\\,Sx,$ where the eigenvalues of $S$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1$" :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Quadratic form I is *not* negative semi-definite since some of the eigenvalues of $Q$ are positive (for example, ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ is positive).\n\n- Quadratic form II is negative semi-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $R$ are non-positive.\n\n- Quadratic form III is negative semi-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $S$ are non-positive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162637 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8385fb50-741c-52a9-b66d-7f14ebf47fdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448786} {:answer/id #uuid "c9d11be8-de44-538d-b685-8fbb9971c1c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448787}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448786} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3395e9e-2ed4-5a34-b396-23c884a38c4d" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449433} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bafa656d-caf1-53a6-b60d-6d69b564a720" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448788} {:answer/id #uuid "1cbd03fa-0bf1-53b5-887b-a59ab5e51a82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448788} :answer-field/id #uuid "870750d0-d594-59b5-bae0-7b99729a4ee6" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449434} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ffbc9d5a-becc-5a3e-a1e6-639ca76f2d57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448790} {:answer/id #uuid "42d3491e-c999-568a-be45-5ecce2260933" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448791}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448791} :answer-field/id #uuid "64499da8-7609-5129-bde6-6636fa8e16a1" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449435}] :question/id #uuid "eb99fd8a-40c2-5ae9-b122-b1e03bf9e31a" :question/math-academy-id "q-94465" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=7$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=10$\n- **II.** ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n- **III.** $A$ is indefinite" :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}9-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 4 & -6-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (9-\\lambda )(-6-\\lambda )-16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -70 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-7,\\,10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Since $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is indefinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269221 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3c25508-a03c-54ed-af75-528b36334d08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464054} {:answer/id #uuid "f997d662-f0fe-5970-be50-6cced7ae7b58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464055}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464055} :answer-field/id #uuid "f09da3cc-6896-57c1-b85f-50c6209f8fae" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464961} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a30af1cd-d4ae-5f09-b616-cad8cd88ec73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464056} {:answer/id #uuid "6256dff6-b788-5cb5-8d29-52c667534b3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464057}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464056} :answer-field/id #uuid "43c4ab03-9594-5b6b-ae8b-d04de998a6a6" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464962} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50467cc1-00c3-5d22-812d-d54f3f3960c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464058} {:answer/id #uuid "3c0b1cda-a69f-5c84-8c20-b1e4a0fb21d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464059}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464058} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3999868-4945-5e38-b4fa-18fc7ca1a6ff" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464963}] :question/id #uuid "30844de8-e636-5e8f-ba19-823c3f8b1cf5" :question/math-academy-id "q-139455" :question/problem "Which of the following quadratic forms over ${R}^{2}$ are negative semi-definite?\n- **I.** ${x}^{T}\\,Qx,$ where the eigenvalues of $Q$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=0$\n- **II.** ${x}^{T}\\,Rx,$ where the eigenvalues of $R$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=1$\n- **III.** ${x}^{T}\\,Sx,$ where the eigenvalues of $S$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Quadratic form I is negative semi-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $Q$ are non-positive.\n\n- Quadratic form II is *not* negative semi-definite since all of the eigenvalues of $R$ are positive.\n\n- Quadratic form III is *not* negative semi-definite since some of the eigenvalues of $S$ are positive (for example, ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ is positive).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269222 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1457b085-9378-5537-a697-8815390a9df5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449520} {:answer/id #uuid "d441aa63-741d-5fc7-bc2f-c1887e1eb78e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449521}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449521} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1f88e3b-6fce-53e2-a863-e2915680b41b" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450714} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca05c6cb-ef8b-5373-a32f-aa4f99251f03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449522} {:answer/id #uuid "cf59c74f-66ed-596d-9946-c21a0fb85247" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449523}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449522} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e10d470-87af-5c03-a536-81b74c55a80e" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450715} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52d5d12d-b0f0-5117-a773-5495feb2d298" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449524} {:answer/id #uuid "00c4db35-c875-59bd-9e1a-40ed34ed91d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449525}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449524} :answer-field/id #uuid "7eefdd5d-b9a9-56fd-912a-519b7219b52b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450716}] :question/id #uuid "e96bcaa6-6221-5f7c-9daa-4047225c2c05" :question/math-academy-id "q-139457" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5$\n- **II.** ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is indefinite\n- **III.** $A$ is negative semi-definite" :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, by computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-(4) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =0,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statements II and III are false. Since both eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is positive semi-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269223 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0174d75c-71dc-5ecb-9978-0afa7bf31836" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464060} {:answer/id #uuid "1571f173-0629-58b6-bf88-d5211c7e4969" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464061}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464061} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8f7c51d-3e18-5bcf-9dfe-c109b3196174" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464964} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf30537c-9bfd-5d49-9bf0-7daf53659951" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464062} {:answer/id #uuid "ad9ec894-c432-504a-9be9-d97512134bd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464063}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464062} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4369eae-3af3-5d0d-96dd-bf3c91d0e50f" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464965} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30828ca0-e286-5320-a545-ecd25568f4d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464064} {:answer/id #uuid "a8b4b2a7-057a-56db-9141-df8234613514" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464065}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464065} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ed51de2-cad2-5793-aada-609d89fdc6cf" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464966}] :question/id #uuid "80bdad61-ebf7-546e-ab4f-0e8486894231" :question/math-academy-id "q-139458" :question/problem "Which of the following quadratic forms over ${R}^{2}$ are positive semi-definite? \n- **I.** ${x}^{T}\\,Qx,$ where the eigenvalues of $Q$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1$\n- **II.** ${x}^{T}\\,Rx,$ where the eigenvalues of $R$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6$\n- **III.** ${x}^{T}\\,Sx,$ where the eigenvalues of $S$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=10$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Quadratic form I is positive semi-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $Q$ are non-negative.\n\n- Quadratic form II is *not* positive semi-definite since some of the eigenvalues of $R$ are negative (for example, ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ is negative).\n\n- Quadratic form III is positive semi-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $S$ are non-negative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269224 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5ddcfc0-292c-5cb2-9f45-5419c2a13d8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449526} {:answer/id #uuid "1ece8954-5a1c-5b2f-8921-c1762576f869" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449527}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449526} :answer-field/id #uuid "0194949c-aad3-5929-accb-a77de79ebda9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450717} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "901712f6-2196-5116-b6eb-9ec322256c84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449528} {:answer/id #uuid "c2001294-60a6-5ba3-bbd0-df63331dc65e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449528} :answer-field/id #uuid "3957c690-352c-5adb-9484-2b219ab8ad73" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450718} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a473654a-3c7a-5cbc-a26c-dd414807f54c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449530} {:answer/id #uuid "9da286f4-dd08-5312-aac1-b131428b4413" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449531}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449531} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd5ff80d-3210-52c8-b24c-ee9e5aec7fcf" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450719}] :question/id #uuid "36a88a67-992a-5e00-85ac-8bb48fc32f6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-139460" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$\n- **II.** ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive semi-definite\n- **III.** $A$ is indefinite" :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-2-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-(4) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -6 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-3,\\,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Since $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is indefinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360498 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abae4a38-c5fd-5f02-a772-5aa129094bed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469734} {:answer/id #uuid "61d28482-9aa5-5496-9fe2-e88afd3b2c83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469735}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469734} :answer-field/id #uuid "1888ce5d-ffbd-5ed4-8be1-6bd4f78e3048" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470479} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c36a82ef-f663-5517-a82d-dfb31295d4df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469736} {:answer/id #uuid "1abaa9d3-1384-5eab-9d1f-4167841b8420" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469737}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469736} :answer-field/id #uuid "78bab090-2cec-52a7-8c1a-ea25848b4605" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470480} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8053ba89-f2f1-5607-8419-7cfe23f95b4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469738} {:answer/id #uuid "2a6d6721-833a-5366-b67a-9b57255478fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469739}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469739} :answer-field/id #uuid "24fa5bdc-7dac-5793-b27a-1eadf77bbfa1" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470481}] :question/id #uuid "1a997acb-de91-5ebd-a6cc-103226757603" :question/math-academy-id "q-94787" :question/problem "Which of the following quadratic forms over ${R}^{2}$ are positive-definite?\n- **I.** ${x}^{T}\\,Qx,$ where the eigenvalues of $Q$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$\n- **II.** ${x}^{T}\\,Rx,$ where the eigenvalues of $R$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-6$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=10$\n- **III.** ${x}^{T}\\,Sx,$ where the eigenvalues of $S$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=8$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Quadratic form I is *not* positive-definite since not all of the eigenvalues of $Q$ are positive (for example, ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ is not positive).\n\n- Quadratic form II is *not* positive-definite since not all of the eigenvalues of $R$ are positive (${\\lambda }_{1}=-6$ is not positive).\n\n- Quadratic form III is positive-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $S$ are positive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360499 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f98de070-157f-534d-80ca-0285e5ea31dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452083} {:answer/id #uuid "174de886-386b-5d7b-a04e-97378dcc50c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452084} :answer-field/id #uuid "e799f149-b778-5e67-89e1-41f4feb99d40" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452889} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e4d1217-ff6e-508b-b3bc-dea5acc63146" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452085} {:answer/id #uuid "78c882b5-c654-506d-9f34-f6de960b4eab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452086}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452086} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb439c2a-82f5-579a-b442-b6cef060ee5e" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452890} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dbe70c7-c5db-5456-9d9f-eceb402243d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452087} {:answer/id #uuid "7f3f8a7a-53c9-58ba-8c93-06d4c7b15b24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452088}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452087} :answer-field/id #uuid "b68b3e83-f797-507a-a054-d24715a897e1" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452891}] :question/id #uuid "13d1d5cf-031d-5ebf-8e4c-d550f344afdd" :question/math-academy-id "q-94795" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5$\n- **II.** ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n- **III.** $A$ is indefinite" :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +5 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =1,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Since all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is positive-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491989 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cdf1f00-26ab-5818-8e31-277f8cd4d2aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491478} {:answer/id #uuid "25731fc9-4c2e-5f16-87e6-dffb67576dc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491531} {:answer/id #uuid "4c35cea2-888a-55d9-90e2-9180209ca85a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491598} {:answer/id #uuid "8e5092a2-21fd-5a49-9b25-5df48bfb2a2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491696} {:answer/id #uuid "9772c9b2-6978-549a-bdda-b075447c40c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491598} :answer-field/id #uuid "f53007b8-0f0f-5ce4-9896-cf1efd1f672a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491980}] :question/id #uuid "0b664be8-b1be-541d-ac3b-8edf1dd9084b" :question/math-academy-id "q-94794" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-7$\n\nII. ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is negative-definite\n\nIII. $A$ is indefinite" :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-6-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-6-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-9 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda -21 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Since $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is indefinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491990 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67a25548-99e2-5e23-b39a-aa2efc11c831" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491640} {:answer/id #uuid "a7e7351a-d152-57ab-844f-b566627dbddf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491748} {:answer/id #uuid "bea842e3-2a89-5931-b9ed-2e94e1710d4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491782} {:answer/id #uuid "e970d694-3cc1-5d9e-89ad-7b1c28b6c89d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491854} {:answer/id #uuid "fde08853-91ee-59b2-94d5-8c3d44748e5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491882}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491640} :answer-field/id #uuid "06fe67e5-af13-5844-be96-3dee54de01cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491888}] :question/id #uuid "10c8a343-4eff-5fd9-bb35-1f19e420eeaf" :question/math-academy-id "q-94791" :question/problem "Which of the following quadratic forms over ${R}^{2}$ are indefinite?\n\nI. ${x}^{T}\\,Qx,$ where the eigenvalues of $Q$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$\n\nII. ${x}^{T}\\,Rx,$ where the eigenvalues of $R$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5$\n\nIII. ${x}^{T}\\,Sx,$ where the eigenvalues of $S$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=6$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1$" :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Quadratic form I is indefinite. Indeed, $Q$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\n- Quadratic form II is *not* indefinite since all of the eigenvalues of $R$ are negative.\n\n- Quadratic form III is indefinite. Indeed, $S$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492017 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0063ed06-ec69-5b0e-8d24-c2348c1e898e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491449} {:answer/id #uuid "3595f10a-b037-5c9e-9a3f-d245c1ebf1e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491560} {:answer/id #uuid "48ba1e0a-ed03-5468-9434-57ed46580151" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491592} {:answer/id #uuid "7ab93efa-8183-5220-b214-84ee09b9d718" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491666} {:answer/id #uuid "a6066e34-528a-5c24-9ea1-fdf66bf3ce42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491743}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491592} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8105dc4-4c7e-5e77-98d9-3c303f190f8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491952}] :question/id #uuid "5a462168-8926-51f0-99cc-2ed457f027f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-139438" :question/problem "Which of the following quadratic forms over ${R}^{2}$ are positive semi-definite? \n\nI. ${x}^{T}\\,Qx,$ where the eigenvalues of $Q$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5$\n\nII. ${x}^{T}\\,Rx,$ where the eigenvalues of $R$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=1$\n\nIII. ${x}^{T}\\,Sx,$ where the eigenvalues of $S$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1$" :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Quadratic form I is *not* positive semi-definite since some of the eigenvalues of $Q$ are negative (for example, ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ is negative).\n\n- Quadratic form II is positive semi-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $R$ are non-negative.\n\n- Quadratic form III is positive semi-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $S$ are non-negative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504680 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "347c0583-8380-5437-bea6-2f27bbcf1b39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504120} {:answer/id #uuid "b7fd3eca-fb7c-56fe-9145-27b9896b8a27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504350} {:answer/id #uuid "e76e528c-2465-542a-9649-85a4f3aa9713" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504432} {:answer/id #uuid "ea32c5e0-4b1f-5fce-827c-bcd543830e52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504438} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb074c9-89ae-5600-83ef-9cf2870ac238" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504477}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504432} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf1da4cf-719f-5bbd-9c32-cdb26adbbe05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504579}] :question/id #uuid "c8c9dba6-d928-5ac1-ba3e-1db8d33cfb50" :question/math-academy-id "q-94793" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 2\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=9$\n\nII. ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n\nIII. $A$ is indefinite" :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 7 \\\\ 7 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-49 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -45 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-5,\\,9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Since $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is indefinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525014 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "342fba80-10c2-55b1-8abf-7ff9c9e264ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524476} {:answer/id #uuid "34e801a5-3875-5ecd-b05f-15545a5c3b9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524480} {:answer/id #uuid "4c8fe2d0-662b-51dd-836f-b0095a0df130" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524512} {:answer/id #uuid "a5a717d0-ad06-5979-9d2e-d3fb8cedc6ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524645} {:answer/id #uuid "df3ce743-bd9f-55c4-aa50-daa5ef48006d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524480} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc3a16ca-ab9d-5910-8403-1994cf7ec17a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524950}] :question/id #uuid "95072baf-8f6f-5ac9-b278-bac347e84d65" :question/math-academy-id "q-94796" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6$\n\nII. ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is negative-definite\n\nIII. $A$ is positive-definite" :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-5-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-5-\\lambda )(-5-\\lambda )-1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+10\\lambda +24 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-6,\\,-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Since all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525025 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12d6028b-8c3e-5aba-826a-b147b99e2f9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524416} {:answer/id #uuid "1b4abab5-df3e-5347-99fb-84fa6de03a5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524432} {:answer/id #uuid "2274a1ba-cb09-551d-a264-6a5d4c0e37fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524443} {:answer/id #uuid "2dffc13b-109f-56f9-902b-d52e5de34dcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524464} {:answer/id #uuid "bca55a76-d282-5f41-9c77-abe46819afe7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524685}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524416} :answer-field/id #uuid "feddc0fa-b88d-5ca9-bd43-99d017ae0768" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524954}] :question/id #uuid "aa5d7a51-2b25-530e-bc34-f2b29d7db7d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-94788" :question/problem "Which of the following quadratic forms over ${R}^{3}$ are negative-definite?\n\nI. ${x}^{T}\\,Qx,$ where the eigenvalues of $Q$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-9$\n\nII. ${x}^{T}\\,Rx,$ where the eigenvalues of $R$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-5$\n\nIII. ${x}^{T}\\,Sx,$ where the eigenvalues of $S$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-6,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=10$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-3$" :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn. \n\n- Quadratic form I is negative-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $Q$ are negative.\n\n- Quadratic form II is *not* negative-definite since not all of the eigenvalues of $R$ are negative (for example, ${\\lambda }_{2}=5$ is not negative).\n\n- Quadratic form III is *not* negative-definite since not all of the eigenvalues of $S$ are negative (${\\lambda }_{2}=10$ is not negative).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Form Type by Calculating the Corresponding Eigenvalues"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162010 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161802 :question/id #uuid "fa658f82-1d0b-54a3-a172-4d59f03f629e" :question/math-academy-id "e-8264" :question/problem "![](images/f1/f1a62bcd57c146902eb55f4a3a631cce69b743c9a3ff7bef659da392a5690476.svg)\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $a_{11}=-6$ and $\\det(A) \\lt 0$\n\nII. $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x}$ is negative-definite\n\nIII. $A$ is positive-definite" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\Delta_i$ for $i=1,2,\\ldots,n$  denote the principal minors of an $n \\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x}$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if $\\Delta_i > 0$ for all $i=1,2,\\ldots,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,\\ldots,n,$ we have $\\Delta_i <          0$ when $i$ is odd, and $\\Delta_i > 0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $a_{11}=-6.$ Expanding $\\det(A)$ along the top row, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-6 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-6)|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}|-(-5)|\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|+1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-6)(-2)+(5)(-7)+12 \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-6<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-6 & -5 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}|=37>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-11<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x},$ and the matrix $A,$ are negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbebdd1b-0716-55cb-8df6-12a35b517ab5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162638 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd7ccdba-167d-52d3-9f65-006af58a47df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448792} {:answer/id #uuid "45e79804-304f-56d7-91a8-584e5bc05ec7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448793}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448793} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fe36113-bf8a-58df-bd27-b2358ccf9634" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449436} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "695c1734-15fe-5bd0-a319-a113359ef2ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448794} {:answer/id #uuid "bb89c867-cc14-5d01-bdda-46476f5b4ce9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448795}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448795} :answer-field/id #uuid "83b596d7-227d-54aa-8cfb-eae069e9bd74" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449437} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf09e997-4a5d-5a14-a4c3-ad0a1321872f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448796} {:answer/id #uuid "09ac96d0-da48-5e6a-a5b5-c11c8827e02c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448797}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448796} :answer-field/id #uuid "8dba44f8-b993-5e8f-8c33-a9da0905d35e" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449438}] :question/id #uuid "9a27d2cf-0561-5715-9600-290d8804741e" :question/math-academy-id "q-94466" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${a}_{11}=9$ and $det(A)=18$\n- **II.** ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n- **III.** $A$ is negative-definite" :question/worked-solution "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=9$ and \n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}|=18.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=9>0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}|=18>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are positive-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162639 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8704f06e-0eca-5a70-9700-c5526052b2a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460407} {:answer/id #uuid "6518d566-4b59-5853-9f01-fa65f41a11a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460408}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460407} :answer-field/id #uuid "328cbc85-02de-5782-9f6f-1bfc8ec8fbb3" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461070} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b050a0c6-7aec-5f7e-8f14-b631435a1e5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460409} {:answer/id #uuid "7dea8288-7e98-5761-9e3b-5108736ab6b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460410}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460410} :answer-field/id #uuid "147acc30-38f6-5dd4-b1fc-756ff6c1cd6a" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461071} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70cc8a99-fd5f-545d-944d-b10871efed96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460411} {:answer/id #uuid "ed4651ad-1ec2-5084-a1f5-126f6778fe6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460412}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460411} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6975a47-d056-596c-bc2f-5293e337fd67" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461072}] :question/id #uuid "1a18cc33-e8b9-51a0-9ec6-b247bdd1e062" :question/math-academy-id "q-94467" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** ${a}_{11}=-1$ and $det(A)>0$\n- **II.** ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is negative-definite\n- **III.** $A$ is positive-definite\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-1$ but \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-1<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{aligned}|=2>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-4<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269225 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "daa79a2c-7393-51fd-aa68-53f7bcbfd36a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449532} {:answer/id #uuid "3d7d5d0c-57fa-5f8d-a238-f9a8292d8c79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449533}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449533} :answer-field/id #uuid "aebff15a-46bb-5e25-b5f7-52cc4f246811" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450720} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1909387-b83c-52db-b6e3-ab497516fded" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449534} {:answer/id #uuid "8eebe797-5a30-55f7-8a3a-e1cba2f6d82d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449535}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449534} :answer-field/id #uuid "79794586-10dc-5ee4-b484-f880ca056d6d" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450721} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08f5b011-a5ec-5e29-8cff-d8e9fe83ea41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449536} {:answer/id #uuid "158c0776-5611-5095-a30f-0b3db7bfac21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449537}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449536} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2987329-85fb-5d6c-a910-7bce43831da5" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450722}] :question/id #uuid "906865b3-32d2-5d7f-b7fc-d38ff3d60bed" :question/math-academy-id "q-139462" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${a}_{11}=2$ and $det(A)=-3$\n- **II.** ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n- **III.** $A$ is negative-definite" :question/worked-solution "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=2$ and \n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}|=-3.\n$$\n\n- Statements II and  III are false. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=2>0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}|=-3<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, neither the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ nor the matrix $A$ is positive-definite or negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360500 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e6b6069-a73e-55b4-8237-8d4f4f222dac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452089} {:answer/id #uuid "db8fa190-5652-57d4-9d6f-36a68e1d279f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452090} :answer-field/id #uuid "88f61762-6f70-5d21-b1c1-800aae0f2fff" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452892} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "311261b6-23c6-5a90-af11-2891a6bef398" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452091} {:answer/id #uuid "3e682b91-127f-5abb-bc3a-e837723de4b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452092}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452092} :answer-field/id #uuid "4558c17e-723a-50fc-8a86-1acb9b4e8d09" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452893} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bedbf47f-c0ac-5fc5-a3f5-ee511e9a5b73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452093} {:answer/id #uuid "1a766f1d-8b11-5027-b338-059d385649a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452094}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452094} :answer-field/id #uuid "bdfd5bb3-65c5-50b4-abd6-2525ca2c0e80" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452894}] :question/id #uuid "81d008a7-fd35-5fff-9f9d-fc0e8e60bfec" :question/math-academy-id "q-94797" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${a}_{11}=-3$ and $det(A)=2$\n- **II.** ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is negative-definite\n- **III.** $A$ is negative-definite" :question/worked-solution "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-3$ and \n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|=2.\n$$\n\n- Statements II and III are true. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-3<0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|=2>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360501 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7c44634-60fd-5951-9be7-dc5e18e58c42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452095} {:answer/id #uuid "82947ba0-932e-5c2a-99e6-18adae0f5f34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452096}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452096} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e7037ca-26f7-5e95-8a8e-f723accdd59c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452895} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d96d178d-0a79-56c3-adbb-d837a0ce5c0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452097} {:answer/id #uuid "209120dd-a131-5d7f-a15d-2260c86df364" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452098}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452098} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3df2f8e-756f-5787-8a56-44336769a7ad" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452896} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40074442-c36f-5a79-91a2-f79a296cd176" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452099} {:answer/id #uuid "c6bd9379-6e9a-5303-ba4e-5e2e9f7337ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452100}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452099} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9ef70e7-a300-569b-994b-335f8013913f" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452897}] :question/id #uuid "95ed5c64-873a-5214-9b7f-9e356847bad2" :question/math-academy-id "q-94801" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${a}_{11}=6$ and $det(A)=17$\n- **II.** ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n- **III.** $A$ is negative-definite" :question/worked-solution "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=6$ and \n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|=17.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=6>0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|=17>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are positive-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360502 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "420ec396-a215-5051-bcb8-10ed9acc95ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452101} {:answer/id #uuid "d7c27e94-c52e-5c96-8d89-053964a8a2d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452102}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452101} :answer-field/id #uuid "83818ab7-8e39-5903-9ee3-959ef2554040" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452898} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c441b03-7a1e-59f5-9515-8053ce5355d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452103} {:answer/id #uuid "cd8eaa01-f5f3-50e0-9b13-165b38fc8277" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452104}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452104} :answer-field/id #uuid "82bbe034-c71c-5eb5-aefd-fd09fd107a27" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452899} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "897837ee-7319-5a6c-a781-a2bf0d97f197" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452105} {:answer/id #uuid "317ed741-0f0a-53c2-9948-7ac37ceef348" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452106}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452106} :answer-field/id #uuid "536fbf80-08b5-5e1a-a2ac-b0eae9c1cf2c" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452900}] :question/id #uuid "43115d1d-8694-5a95-b413-4600fbd2bc68" :question/math-academy-id "q-94804" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${a}_{11}=-5$ and $det(A)>0$\n- **II.** ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is negative-definite\n- **III.** $A$ is negative-definite" :question/worked-solution "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-5$ but \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-8)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-8)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-8<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statements II and III are true. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-5<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}|=1>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-8<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360503 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e082f9d-d667-53bf-8ebe-ea0165b6dc26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452107} {:answer/id #uuid "1b961c92-afa7-5358-90fd-24c675c9bd2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452108}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452107} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3d091f6-4c30-55e8-95e8-5336bea05763" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452901} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e233fee2-70e2-5e90-896f-04fbc826023e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452109} {:answer/id #uuid "a851a8d9-1e67-5532-b8dd-92c9d9b65acc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452110}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452110} :answer-field/id #uuid "3be5e419-a12d-5643-8194-2d9a2b4897ec" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452902} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0fe63a9-b90e-5777-b826-3d8827fd649a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452111} {:answer/id #uuid "1aded71f-8051-53a7-97fa-e199f9608ed3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452112}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452111} :answer-field/id #uuid "42458164-cc6a-5528-9e47-6288386413d8" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452903}] :question/id #uuid "7d5f731a-8eca-5400-88d7-dd10fc541120" :question/math-academy-id "q-94805" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${a}_{11}=8$ and $det(A)<0$\n- **II.** ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n- **III.** $A$ is negative-definite" :question/worked-solution "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=8$ but \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}8 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}8 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =21>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=8>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}8 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}|=7>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=21>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are positive-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360504 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fea1b93a-38ba-5c72-9bee-3fbe580f05cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452113} {:answer/id #uuid "81ba5de2-4ec0-55fe-bab8-33d6d9253f04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452114}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452114} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f57c56b-b2e1-58e7-8141-026fa0dded98" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452904} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85c12093-8dda-5103-9819-5b62a8cc94b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452115} {:answer/id #uuid "d2e0ca83-7bc5-54f5-b90f-2bcba9b76ad1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452116}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452115} :answer-field/id #uuid "2dc9ccf9-fe82-5943-aefd-c2e968dec187" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452905} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c2dacb8-3a73-5142-9d28-821696fba02d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452117} {:answer/id #uuid "07437bee-0a6c-5947-bc81-ab31e28f0bc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452118}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452118} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa4392f3-f738-5e5a-88d9-29ab52adafd4" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452906}] :question/id #uuid "b0bcde67-e6f1-5859-87e0-ff664264d2ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-94806" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${a}_{11}=-6$ and $det(A)<0$\n- **II.** ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n- **III.** $A$ is negative-definite" :question/worked-solution "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-6$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-6<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-6<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}|=2>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-6<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491984 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ea94f50-c7a7-5689-a3e2-07536e394575" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491602} {:answer/id #uuid "619e8956-a4dc-535d-9bbc-e8f309b768c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491629} {:answer/id #uuid "ba8db3ff-eee5-5fbf-93b0-295940eacd17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491775} {:answer/id #uuid "c37deb26-59ff-5b78-9971-a3b1623542f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491790} {:answer/id #uuid "d2235f79-7130-5140-b61b-f3ad01e68069" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491811} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a2c4d76-d1ed-5ea1-9132-eb93755f0b15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491922}] :question/id #uuid "06dbce8a-d24c-5a95-a85b-452eb260bee0" :question/math-academy-id "q-139463" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${a}_{11}=1$ and $det(A)<0$\n\nII. ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n\nIII. $A$ is negative-definite" :question/worked-solution "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that ${a}_{11}=1,$ but \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=1>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|=1>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=1>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are positive-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524999 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c770309-60dd-57d9-8388-da614d588229" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524434} {:answer/id #uuid "3543723c-355b-52c7-b10b-104509d59ebd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524481} {:answer/id #uuid "5585210e-e5b7-562b-96ab-a025b6173eec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524530} {:answer/id #uuid "a1ff6a24-e096-5a38-adf3-8f6baa63dc90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524638} {:answer/id #uuid "f936acee-b9ba-5a2d-aab8-0d02b6a64ffc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524827}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524827} :answer-field/id #uuid "079db36a-f121-5453-be8e-d40d62051237" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524844}] :question/id #uuid "7aa930b4-3c77-5008-aaf5-9bbf298703c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-94800" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${a}_{11}=-7$ and $det(A)=-3$\n\nII. ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is negative-definite\n\nIII. $A$ is positive-definite" :question/worked-solution "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-7$ and \n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}|=3.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-7<0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}|=3>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Form Type Using Sylvester's Criterion"}] :topic/math-academy-id 3127 :topic/title "Positive-Definite and Negative-Definite Quadratic Forms"}] [{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125440} :activity/id #uuid "9faeb18a-39f2-528b-927a-5e59d0f90fc7" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125440 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125799 :tutorial/content "Let's suppose we have the linear map\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T: {\\color{blue}V} \\to {\\color{red}W}\n$$\n\nwhere the bases of the vector spaces $\\color{blue}V$ and ${\\color{red}W}$ respectively are given by\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}=\\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{b}_1},{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{b}_2}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{b}_3} \\}, \\qquad \\qquad {\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}} = \\{ {\\color{red}\\mathbf{c}_1},{\\color{red}\\mathbf{c}_2} \\}.\n$$\n\nThe **matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ with respect to the bases $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}$** is the matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $\\mathbf{T}({\\color{blue}\\mathbf{b}_1}),$ $\\mathbf{T}(\\color{blue}{\\mathbf{b}_2}),$ and $\\mathbf{T}({\\color{blue}\\mathbf{b}_3})$ relative to the basis ${\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{B|C} & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ [T({b}_{1})]_{C} & [T({b}_{2})]_{C} & [T({b}_{3})]_{C} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, if \n\n![](images/be/be8cff88ef83ebc67d0df7a50a243c55c8f11aa92e7a07f64b73bb30ed701392.svg)\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{B|C} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 10 & -12 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "da91373e-0973-56a4-91c9-aa2a2b1de0b1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3589 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9ee24e02-1f78-5b25-8d1c-f19b4a0052ee" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125546}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125441 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124542 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84947238-f446-5261-8ecd-ee263f64e04f"} :step/id #uuid "d4cf88b7-d61d-5b12-a8f6-f786898cb8c3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125442}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125442 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124543 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8725caed-481c-5fbe-a0c4-cffdcb308881"} :step/id #uuid "80774b4b-be41-5637-b9a9-f728e8911827"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125546 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124603 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7f1e542-f0a2-54ea-ba7f-0a9928415907"} :step/id #uuid "a48f8724-cd55-576f-8431-f47c3d90338a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125547}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125547 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124604 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0587fad6-5ef2-5200-9b60-ddbd0bc21ba7"} :step/id #uuid "f3a9b6e8-c0c7-5e91-a3fa-8081e91b886a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125548}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125548 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125843 :tutorial/content "Given the matrix $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}|\\mathcal{C}}$ of a linear map $\\mathbf{T}:V \\to W$ relative to the bases $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C},$ and the coordinates $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ of a vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to $\\mathcal{B},$ the image of $\\mathbf{x}$ under $\\mathbf{T}$ with respect to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$ can be found as \n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{x})]_{\\mathcal{C}} = \n[\\mathbf{T}]_{{\\mathcal{B}}|{\\mathcal{C}}}\\cdot [\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}\n$$\n\nFor example, if\n\n![](images/e3/e3623ffb88602004ad3ab01f55976d3a55759563a6088f4e2a10b7b8849a6f70.svg)\n\nthen the image of $\\mathbf x$ under $\\mathbf T$ relative to $\\mathcal C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{C} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "db73b6f4-55d4-5d30-856e-463bc6cc9964" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5093 :tutorial/title "The Image of a Vector Under a Linear Map Given by a Matrix Relative to Two Bases"} :step/id #uuid "14e61c95-f43b-5c3d-8e38-75c4db0838d1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125441}}] :activity/title "The Matrix of a Linear Map Relative to Two Bases" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124752} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501244} :activity/id #uuid "151e5f91-3cb4-5ced-a9bd-b1b2446d0e25" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501244 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501158 :question/id #uuid "3a497a8c-181f-5ddb-8e0c-d7f0dcc66829"} :step/id #uuid "0408ea5d-a169-5ec2-b15a-fbb75152c5cb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501248}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501248 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501135 :question/id #uuid "00e54e6a-cd0f-51b4-97b3-2489c07dd2c2"} :step/id #uuid "0b3a2889-b1be-57f0-91ef-41940e81fc0b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501265}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501257 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362216 :question/id #uuid "c64bcdb5-768e-5293-a151-ad4d8fbb8250"} :step/id #uuid "236b321d-1506-5f60-8283-c253a6c03092" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501276}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501265 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501217 :question/id #uuid "ba29e43e-8fe2-5bbe-a64e-366d83a4fca9"} :step/id #uuid "467923da-fcad-50c7-8110-cff41e775129" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501257}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501276 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362217 :question/id #uuid "29ca0842-b657-584e-9ec2-fc1fbb7af353"} :step/id #uuid "7e3e85bd-ebee-5a59-b335-848da96d3843"}] :activity/title "The Matrix of a Linear Map Relative to Two Bases" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500459}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186048582 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124542 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124351 :question/id #uuid "e9539818-ad55-5bb3-8c5d-5b85303f9e6d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5521" :question/problem "![](images/9f/9f39d604d602f4813ded4f4604e70ee5ca21e8b4f8a53c059dfb61db9d1623ba.svg)\n\nSuppose $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ are bases of the vector spaces $V$ and $W,$ respectively. The matrix $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}|\\mathcal{C}}$ of the linear map $\\mathbf{T}:V \\rightarrow W$ relative to these bases, and the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in V$ relative to $\\mathcal{B}$ are shown above. Find $[\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{x})]_{\\mathcal{C}}.$" :question/worked-solution "The coordinates of the image of $\\mathbf{x}$ under $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$ can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{C} & =[T]_{B|C}\\cdot [x]_{B} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84947238-f446-5261-8ecd-ee263f64e04f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125124 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ebab81ca-1d47-583d-8baf-2f28baf95aaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123345} {:answer/id #uuid "f773fa5c-2420-5867-b57e-645e35abfbf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123346} {:answer/id #uuid "13e8da75-c9d3-53e1-b686-d0f2b12d284c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123347} {:answer/id #uuid "2bb920bf-6c15-5c79-b749-c7337a75d7f0" :answer/type {: