$B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ of the vector space $V$ and the basis $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ given by\n\n$$\nC=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$\n\nFind the matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the map $T:V\\to {R}^{2}$ relative to these bases if \n\n$$\nT({b}_{1})=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,T({b}_{2})=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,T({b}_{3})=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $[T]_{B|C}$ is the matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nFirst, we need to find the coordinates of $T({b}_{i})$ relative to the basis $C$ for each $i=1,2,3.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the following matrix:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) & T({b}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 1 & -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -8 & 2 & -6\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -5 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the right-hand side gives the matrix of the linear transformation relative to the bases $B$ and $C:$\n\n$$\n[T]_{B|C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362214 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "116083c6-8fa2-5e8a-9cd7-946083523118" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value