atrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ what are the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\n- From the matrix $C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -(-2) & 1\\end{bmatrix},$ we deduce that $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=1$ and $\\text{Im}(\\lambda )=-2.$ So, our eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda =1+(-2)\\text{i}=1-2\\text{i}.\n$$\n\n- From the matrix $P=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix},$ we deduce that our corresponding eigenvector is\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}0+1\\text{i} \\\\ 2+0\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda ,$ and the second eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nTherefore, our eigenvalues and eigenvectors are \n\n$$\n\\lambda =1∓2\\text{i},\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562465 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dd09250-36e7-55f0-82b8-a9e7a77f1438" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =3\\pm \\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}1\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ -1\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561702} {:answer/id #uuid "889ef78d-7759-5373-abe0-8c890bd54b6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =1\\pm 3\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ -1\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id