d #edb/ref 17592186388750}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388749} :answer-field/id #uuid "66b0905b-9058-5e15-92b6-332aab984e53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388745}] :question/id #uuid "1a9407fa-0156-51d0-868b-a6bb68a8909c" :question/math-academy-id "q-140498" :question/problem "$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\text{i}-1 \\\\ \\text{i}+2\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2+3\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\ne C,\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda })\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{―}{\\lambda }=2-3\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{―}{v},$ the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1-2\\text{i} \\\\ 2-\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395651 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4219457-908d-5c1d-94f7-a499e34c89f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395653} {:answer/id #uuid "33f641a6-0347-55ab-a00e-a1cf599920f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395654} {:answer/id #uuid "1df9a6d3-1b83-5494-b0c4-ae30de9f971c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395655} {:answer/id #uuid "4bddbcd7-63e5-581b-96a9-465fedef0af9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395656} {:answer/id #uuid "4b47ce83-6409-5bb4-ae4a-a2098cb1ee80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395653} :answer-field/id #uuid "a74f3e2c-c836-57fa-b4f6-b5ef43f0c419" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395652}] :question/id #uuid "d1be07fe-c7c3-5b64-8061-f1dc9ad8baf8" :question/math-academy-id "q-99412" :question/problem "$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1-\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3-2\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\ne C,\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda })\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{―}{\\lambda }=3+2\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{―}{v},$ the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2-\\text{i} \\\\ 1+\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395665 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e196512e-1c8e-5187-b969-8d1f8f8d5c47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395667} {:answer/id #uuid "3dcd7441-6b21-57de-a31c-17ca6a0f1cda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395668} {:answer/id #uuid "ee707b83-d226-5ed6-baf9-f30ac78abcc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395669} {:answer/id #uuid "6a5b4235-cc67-53b5-a8a9-129a5d3f6ab6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395670} {:answer/id #uuid "99e977e9-1f82-5a4e-93e5-ff723aa90c12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395671}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395671} :answer-field/id #uuid "619fbace-2c7b-50b1-a0f2-8ac4398e4712" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395666}] :question/id #uuid "57e563fd-7adc-5039-9d3f-4d9ce896a93d" :question/math-academy-id "q-99419" :question/problem "$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-3+\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 7 \\\\ -7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-7\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\ne C,\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda })\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 7 \\\\ -7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{―}{\\lambda }=7\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{―}{v},$ the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3-\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410856 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a822e0b-1fa5-5287-afb1-796b6d1e0af8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410833} {:answer/id #uuid "39b2aad9-d90d-5e0c-99c0-086cce1f5858" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410838} {:answer/id #uuid "b4f2b609-e44a-59ef-8d01-1bf7e729ec81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410843} {:answer/id #uuid "b6dd3430-18a3-5d4c-aa84-56246e5e6e49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410844} {:answer/id #uuid "d396ac8a-9e62-5ef7-aa22-08d3e2840f8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410844} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba30525c-f325-567a-9297-5b7c22b96813" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410853}] :question/id #uuid "b89eb3d0-aaac-5e32-8106-610f887f220d" :question/math-academy-id "q-99416" :question/problem "$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\text{i} \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1+3\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\ne C,\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda })\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{―}{\\lambda }=-1-3\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{―}{v},$ the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\text{i} \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}{{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Using the Complex Conjugate"}] :topic/math-academy-id 2018 :topic/title "Reducing Real 2x2 Matrices to Rotation-Scaling Form"}] [{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176738} :activity/id #uuid "f3ece41b-4cc9-59dc-9bd4-33a0b27a6bb1" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176738 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176998 :tutorial/content "Every square matrix $A$ can be written as\n\n$$\nA = PJP^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form and $P$ is an invertible matrix. \n\nExpressing $A$ in the above form is called a **Jordan canonical decomposition** of $A,$ and is an extension of diagonalization that works for all square matrices.\n\nIn the case [math]A[/math] is  a [math]2 \\times 2[/math] matrix, the matrix [math]J[/math]  has only one of the following forms:\n\n1.  [math]\\qquad J = J_1(\\lambda_1) {\\:\\oplus\\:} J_1(\\lambda_2) = \\begin{bmatrix} \\lambda_1 &0\\\\ 0 & \\lambda_2\n\\end{bmatrix}[/math]\n\n2.   [math]\\qquad J = J_2(\\lambda)  = \\begin{bmatrix} \\lambda &0\\\\ 0 & \\lambda\n\\end{bmatrix}[/math] \n\nSo, to find a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A$ means to find both \n\n- a Jordan canonical form $J,$ and\n\n- an invertible matrix $P$ that satisfies the equation $A = PJP^{-1}$.\n\nIf $A$ is diagonalizable, finding a Jordan canonical decomposition is the same as diagonalizing. In this lesson, we will focus on non-diagonalizable matrices." :tutorial/id #uuid "b78c03ae-4f75-5e2c-8ecb-075a951ef7ba" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5440 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d85ecef4-7b11-572a-acbf-f07454e816b0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176779}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176739 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175989 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e22ff208-ff10-5106-8922-26adc9007e40"} :step/id #uuid "9fe89afc-113b-5dd4-9b1a-24dbc6a5f9cd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176740}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176740 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175990 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac67bb72-7802-547d-b9d9-ab238cc3768f"} :step/id #uuid "cf6a9020-5da3-5879-aafb-e5abf137f695"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176776 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176010 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "53e40a15-120c-5587-979b-cc1369d244bf"} :step/id #uuid "7a2b6662-176d-57cd-81a3-1f0805f81c10" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176777}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176777 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177016 :tutorial/content "Let's find the Jordan canonical decomposition of the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find a Jordan canonical decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we follow a three-step **Jordan decomposition algorithm:**\n\n**Step 1:** Find the eigenvalues of the matrix.\n\nWe solve the characteristic equation $\\vert A - \\lambda I \\vert = 0.$ For the given matrix, there is only one eigenvalue, namely $\\lambda = -2.$\n\n**Step 2:** Find a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ consisting of the generalized eigenvectors corresponding to each eigenvalue.\n\nApplying the usual method for computing eigenvectors, we get an eigenvector \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\ncorresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = -2.$ This is the first vector of our basis.\n\nTo find the second vector of our basis, we have to find a generalized eigenvector ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v_2}}$ of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda = -2.$ So, our vectors ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{v_1}}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v_2}}$ must form the following chain:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v_2}} \\xrightarrow{A+2I} {\\color{red}\\mathbf{v_1}} \\xrightarrow{A+2I}  \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to find ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2}$ we must solve the equation\n\n$$\n(A +2I ){\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} = {\\color{red}\\mathbf{v_1}}.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation using our usual methods gives us the solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNotice that the set $\\left\\{ {\\color{red}\\mathbf{v_1}}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v_2}}\\right\\}$ is linearly independent and thus forms a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\n**Step 3: ** Construct the matrices $J$ and $P.$\n\nNow that we have our special basis, we can construct a Jordan canonical form $J$ and invertible matrix $P.$ \nThe matrix $J$ is represented by a single $2 \\times 2$ Jordan block:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, the columns of $P$ are the vectors $\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2,$ where the first column *must* be the eigenvector ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{v_1}}.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, the Jordan canonical decomposition of $A$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** In Jordan decomposition, the order in which we write our vectors in the matrix $P$ is important!  The first column of $P$ must *always* be the eigenvector." :tutorial/id #uuid "7af5f830-7b7a-5816-8e89-725dde4d7e35" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5592 :tutorial/title "Finding the Jordan Decomposition of a Non-Diagonalizable 2x2 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "515aec85-7852-5795-bf28-261943fbef4c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176739}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176779 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177018 :tutorial/content "As an example, consider the following matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis matrix has a single eigenvalue $\\lambda = {\\color{red}2}$ and, since $A$ is not scalar, it is not diagonalizable. Thus, the Jordan canonical form of $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can show that $A$ has the Jordan canonical decomposition $A =  PJP^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe main focus of this lesson is to find a matrix [math]P[/math] for any non-diagonalizable [math]2\\times 2[/math] matrix [math]A[/math] that satisfies the equation [math]A = PJP^{-1}.[/math]\n\nNotice that the columns of $P$ form a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ that generates the following chains: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe vectors ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1}$ and ${\\color{DarkBlue}\\mathbf{v}_2}$ are generalized eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda = {\\color{red}2}.$ Here, ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1}$ is an eigenvector of $A,$ while ${\\color{DarkBlue}\\mathbf{v}_2}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2.$ \n\nIn general, the matrix $P$ of any Jordan canonical decomposition of a non-diagonalizable $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda$ must satisfy the following property:\n\n- The columns of $P$ associated with a Jordan block $J_2(\\lambda)$ are generalized eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda,$ where\n\n- the first column of $P$ is an eigenvector, and\n\n- the second column of $P$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ that is mapped to the first column under the action of $A-\\lambda I.$\n\n**Watch out!** The order of the columns of $P$ is important! The first column should *always* be an eigenvector." :tutorial/id #uuid "a948c73d-7422-50c9-8bb6-610ce5e3ef02" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5601 :tutorial/title "An Introductory Example"} :step/id #uuid "bd700e86-b856-5a98-b936-fa4661c1893e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176776}}] :activity/title "Jordan Canonical Decomposition of a 2x2 Matrix" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176163}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186048638 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175989 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175840 :question/id #uuid "53bf6a59-06ab-5597-999c-20f7ca55bba2" :question/math-academy-id "e-9088" :question/problem "![](images/8a/8a5b765f60afdb05946a2ed393c2776c532fca4c2905969928b8dc66b55d8913.png)\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A = P J P^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $\\dfrac{a}{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=4.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ corresponds to an eigenvector of the eigenvalue $\\lambda=4.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-4I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}-(4)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1=-x_2.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n![](images/ca/ca51c47bc0e065fa8ec1303f235f5b5e0c818bf271abbfb90ba96219fd6dd91a.png)\n\nTherefore, setting $x_2=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n![](images/34/34e1d2e3dcdfd3c46c3e2ca5360fd596e307efe042f6676413736a100d058155.png)\n\nFinally, $\\dfrac{a}{b}=\\dfrac{-1}{1} = -1.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e22ff208-ff10-5106-8922-26adc9007e40" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176227 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83b1a1b8-d8cf-5347-9d35-6ec9e289471e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173621} {:answer/id #uuid "cd70ffe8-8065-59c1-82f7-24f8bc59ffdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173622} {:answer/id #uuid "e3ac2593-fa81-53e4-bd6f-83e7aab1248b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173623} {:answer/id #uuid "1d16ab94-7758-5bd1-a863-00bb9496df7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173624} {:answer/id #uuid "41e9a759-b88c-5924-a5f1-9ac1f16ca4ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173625}] :answer-field/id #uuid "7bc387fa-dfb8-5acf-bf31-6981269c1b8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173620}] :question/id #uuid "d31d630a-ed7a-54dd-ba30-39be1481e74c" :question/math-academy-id "q-108732" :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, J = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}, P = \\begin{bmatrix}a & ∗ \\\\ b & ∗\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A = PJP^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A$, find the value of $\\frac{a}{b}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176234 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4cbc8393-55a3-5dd7-aa83-981c2d75f796" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241865} {:answer/id #uuid "670ba11a-afae-5ba3-a37b-c98b26f726e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242109} {:answer/id #uuid "7073b6fc-c6c4-5311-a0c9-5a3bc467c08f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242183} {:answer/id #uuid "879029da-758b-5ccd-a2b7-4bef775ff8eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242368} {:answer/id #uuid "d9f1b0c6-570c-5c3f-b7e5-f10192813eaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243122}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242183} :answer-field/id #uuid "27dd6a4d-8a50-5772-9c72-07370fafa09a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243510}] :question/id #uuid "addb688e-b364-54ad-8e80-207a23ed2756" :question/math-academy-id "q-109368" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,J=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}a & ∗ \\\\ b & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{b}{a}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=7.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ corresponds to an eigenvector of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =7.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-7I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-7I & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}-(7)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{1}{-2}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244275 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56eed2f1-fd0e-5b06-9752-53993795c17e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241967} {:answer/id #uuid "8553cd39-135b-5a59-b422-16be0f27db0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242346} {:answer/id #uuid "904e58bc-edb1-58bd-a398-3ea676a042a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242444} {:answer/id #uuid "bc02b1ed-f555-5938-a516-885b8b14a31a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242828} {:answer/id #uuid "d69f94fc-18e3-5521-b416-48d706c5c693" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243085}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242346} :answer-field/id #uuid "9edf1c90-46d5-5982-a05c-1f4a82cad8f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243734}] :question/id #uuid "b3283580-5a59-5591-b6e2-a7b4ead9a8ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-109363" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,J=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}a & ∗ \\\\ b & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{a}{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ corresponds to an eigenvector of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-(-3)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-3)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-(-3)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244276 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14f3897d-d06b-57f3-9fc4-287fc2b7e316" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241360} {:answer/id #uuid "1c5ca837-abc8-5cc0-a210-34c44f7c7e43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241419} {:answer/id #uuid "210da64c-6bff-5a92-93f3-642e19e7c5f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241468} {:answer/id #uuid "44abda93-7c4e-52e6-88a7-469e2a9b9e6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241797} {:answer/id #uuid "a4108a85-e71a-50fb-abea-05dd9b2415d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242619}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241360} :answer-field/id #uuid "136142f2-58fc-5a31-b81e-20427ac7d4ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243469}] :question/id #uuid "d21971e7-3553-5c30-b33e-2ba71b3dd2f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-109364" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,J=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}a & ∗ \\\\ b & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{b}{a}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ corresponds to an eigenvector of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-2I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-(2)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244277 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ca8a1f1-9996-5c46-8acc-7a0b415b7674" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241288} {:answer/id #uuid "16e4870c-1d6a-5063-92a3-51b3f778712d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241377} {:answer/id #uuid "624b6cfe-aa71-5d5c-b310-ad6a13fec89f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242062} {:answer/id #uuid "80697a8f-2a83-5893-a1bf-3f0a51054138" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242306} {:answer/id #uuid "94944fc1-9f41-5879-a03c-917ea32bd8d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242484}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241288} :answer-field/id #uuid "811612a2-19e3-5c4c-8627-82394c1c7417" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243686}] :question/id #uuid "bd78e648-49a9-5a9b-9b15-2651f4d8a066" :question/math-academy-id "q-109365" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,J=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}a & ∗ \\\\ b & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{b}{a}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=1.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ corresponds to an eigenvector of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{1}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244278 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00a47920-79b5-5dcb-9b61-f663d9892670" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241191} {:answer/id #uuid "8070a8b5-97c8-5a43-9bee-6985b4632e80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242307} {:answer/id #uuid "8eb7addd-ca4b-5a7f-952a-76b997e9e5d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242435} {:answer/id #uuid "b6dea218-fa3f-5b60-b84f-b05f9e7da9b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242782} {:answer/id #uuid "b7d7bd42-bc48-59af-a2a1-cc6a84d86a2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242790}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242790} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a768823-860f-5913-b915-573dee9df4ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243580}] :question/id #uuid "10df4b8a-61e0-5697-aa9a-7ba2145d58ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-109366" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,J=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}a & ∗ \\\\ b & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{a}{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=4.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ corresponds to an eigenvector of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-4I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-(4)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{-1}{1}=-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244279 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "397a560d-a961-5792-8aad-025750cdbbdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241693} {:answer/id #uuid "433ac2a6-743b-592c-95d0-b7dc9a20f662" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241785} {:answer/id #uuid "577f45d7-3389-50c6-90af-1541dc2c0744" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241974} {:answer/id #uuid "7082a5df-dd94-5938-a951-44f3c028d218" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242184} {:answer/id #uuid "91b445e8-48d9-5e09-83c9-56492f9020a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242460} :answer-field/id #uuid "537bd975-c2a5-5f08-9e2b-066a72315c27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243601}] :question/id #uuid "81af8621-a6f2-5d84-bac1-909566895a26" :question/math-academy-id "q-109369" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,J=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}a & ∗ \\\\ b & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{b}{a}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=7.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ corresponds to an eigenvector of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =7.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-7I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-7I & =\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-(7)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{1}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244280 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "290c0cdd-c87b-55b3-9870-cae9c04402fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241539} {:answer/id #uuid "3e6f0503-833d-5111-94ea-869a2eef37fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241738} {:answer/id #uuid "5812afad-b7ca-5b25-b0bc-d81028a8a891" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241978} {:answer/id #uuid "85f3a46b-125b-51f4-8d5d-b4a19fd05445" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242349} {:answer/id #uuid "b3b2406f-27a5-56ac-940b-151e940c6d51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242747}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241978} :answer-field/id #uuid "28bc2d12-6eab-558f-8731-57a0736b9073" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243512}] :question/id #uuid "f4fbea93-a5d1-546f-b6d9-0bc890e1d4f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-109371" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -8 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,J=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}a & ∗ \\\\ b & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{a}{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ corresponds to an eigenvector of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-3I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -8 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}-(3)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{2}{1}=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380545 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3006e0c2-ef52-5f3c-9955-c9d176473b67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380547} {:answer/id #uuid "2c785bde-763d-54ab-80a2-f8a40afee48f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380548} {:answer/id #uuid "75e19b02-1c3d-5b9c-87ba-8015af946524" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380549} {:answer/id #uuid "ef4abef2-98cf-5949-8dbd-07e6f8e93e14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380550} {:answer/id #uuid "fd0046a7-b220-56a1-867c-b146b55368f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380551}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380550} :answer-field/id #uuid "46812f21-d9bc-590d-abb9-823360888eff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380546}] :question/id #uuid "75d3e9ed-a5bd-5596-ad98-d12c44041497" :question/math-academy-id "q-109342" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,J=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}a & ∗ \\\\ b & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{a}{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ corresponds to an eigenvector of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-3I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-(3)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{-1}{1}=-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386418 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b855d3e8-e95e-5c35-894a-971c5f883424" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386420} {:answer/id #uuid "abbbb29c-b4a3-5f49-b7aa-6ac457e198f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386421} {:answer/id #uuid "c972a1ee-2eef-5660-94d3-7fa98d4afe5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386422} {:answer/id #uuid "576ba8e6-5802-51cc-997a-18de8b1bd635" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386423} {:answer/id #uuid "ef984826-6f0e-57be-a40f-4fc59b0d23a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386424}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386423} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd0ed91e-60e6-59f1-8a3a-e73e06801f99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386419}] :question/id #uuid "8455efb1-e98c-5036-9b81-c46428ea0a75" :question/math-academy-id "q-109367" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,J=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}a & ∗ \\\\ b & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{b}{a}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=5.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ corresponds to an eigenvector of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-5I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-(5)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{1}{-1}=-1.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Elements of a Jordan Canonical Decomposition for a 2x2 Matrix: The First Column Case"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175990 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175841 :question/id #uuid "90e87dca-f423-5587-b39a-5d6e931db18e" :question/math-academy-id "e-9089" :question/problem "![](images/35/35e38dfcccab52240fa316b41acacf74548e7218a5d79d856f69c947f3d26394.png)\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A = P J P^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the second column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=4.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that \n\n- the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector ![](images/9b/9bff79e13c06645253e57b8e6f43689c7ce21ac818c3ffdc677e03b5a9b08eb7.png) of the eigenvalue $\\lambda=4,$\n\n- the second column of $P$ corresponds to the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$ such that $(A-4I)\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\mathbf{v}_1.$\n\nNow, computing $A-4I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}-(4)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(A - 4I)\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\mathbf{v}_1$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 5 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1=-\\dfrac 15 - x_2.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n![](images/44/44f1c49ffc09942f7062c8e4da4419a214551cdf659ee92be03556c52a2f3005.png)\n\nTherefore, setting $x_2=0,$ we get  \n\n![](images/e2/e2ad676d41fff87d0c519bf6cbb369a5ec9ac209c8b2b9353a658d9307d6109d.png)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac67bb72-7802-547d-b9d9-ab238cc3768f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176226 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e4c3d89-8e82-5e94-8553-f7576a522ff3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241590} {:answer/id #uuid "32c29cfa-d018-56aa-9c36-e1748434378a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241627} {:answer/id #uuid "3f871c16-7231-5aa7-8b25-2c8d8977dd8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241749} {:answer/id #uuid "ed459052-ae4e-5b90-9927-4c723f550f89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243277} {:answer/id #uuid "ffe73031-ee52-5e07-b7bb-dce8bbf5607e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243434}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243434} :answer-field/id #uuid "19850188-a340-5c8f-97c0-e9bf236b7f0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243483}] :question/id #uuid "b0fdb675-21a9-5763-9fe7-cb59859241e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-108728" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,J=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & a \\\\ 1 & b\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $a-2b.$" :question/worked-solution "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that \n\n- the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3,$\n\n- the second column of $P$ corresponds to the vector ${v}_{2}$ such that $(A-3I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}.$\n\nNow, computing $A-3I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}-(3)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(A-3I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-1+2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1+2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get  \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $a-2b=1-2\\cdot 1=-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176235 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62c0d323-0b1e-5af4-aa03-4da2f051cf85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242065} {:answer/id #uuid "640710b8-6d39-552c-84f9-ce095aaf9c1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242080} {:answer/id #uuid "72baafc5-623c-511e-985a-b99d772a949b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242196} {:answer/id #uuid "df164383-88f3-5584-acb5-d35f4b81f20d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243163} {:answer/id #uuid "f7a185fe-a32f-58e6-a48c-4e8d0f27f4d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243367}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243163} :answer-field/id #uuid "55b8e95a-bab0-5411-bb11-9cd8d7bdd103" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243608}] :question/id #uuid "71955644-7fce-58e7-aa23-c8e43615d89a" :question/math-academy-id "q-109381" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,J=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & a \\\\ 1 & b\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $a-2b.$" :question/worked-solution "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=1.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that \n\n- the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1,$\n\n- the second column of $P$ corresponds to the vector ${v}_{2}$ such that $(A-I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}.$\n\nNow, computing $A-I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(A-I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=1+2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1+2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get  \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $a-2b=3-2\\cdot 1=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244281 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27ee5025-4ebd-5f5a-b5e4-09b9ead9ca29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241528} {:answer/id #uuid "5949ca32-c191-5120-9f04-4ac30b8e563a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241984} {:answer/id #uuid "b3153140-5ee9-5209-8c08-5a8f698db225" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242739} {:answer/id #uuid "ce5717ac-5b74-529f-8168-42712e86525b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242996} {:answer/id #uuid "e051ef75-4ccb-5426-89e1-8839c66a7e41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241528} :answer-field/id #uuid "67fbff3a-f876-5971-a21c-709b88cf04be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243645}] :question/id #uuid "f162f698-8220-54f4-a16d-68bf7f2cabee" :question/math-academy-id "q-109373" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,J=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & a \\\\ 1 & b\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $2a+2b.$" :question/worked-solution "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that \n\n- the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2,$\n\n- the second column of $P$ corresponds to the vector ${v}_{2}$ such that $(A-2I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}.$\n\nNow, computing $A-2I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}-(2)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(A-2I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get  \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $2a+2b=2\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{2})+2\\cdot 1=-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244282 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d650a01-a27c-5335-b5d5-77250c8b217f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241428} {:answer/id #uuid "210caf65-f8af-5bc5-a610-03f9a3aba6cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241467} {:answer/id #uuid "274c48cd-1d04-5c71-b2cd-ce1f60186746" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241520} {:answer/id #uuid "3d03ae0e-2813-5de4-a501-c84bea6e85c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241723} {:answer/id #uuid "edcae696-33fd-5955-9ad6-d0be7748e8dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241467} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5e367f5-8a3a-560d-8854-d60c90a7dbc6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243866}] :question/id #uuid "ba91cd3e-3266-5b35-800f-87d378a05f51" :question/math-academy-id "q-109377" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,J=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & a \\\\ 2 & b\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $6a-3b.$" :question/worked-solution "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that \n\n- the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3,$\n\n- the second column of $P$ corresponds to the vector ${v}_{2}$ such that $(A-(-3)I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}.$\n\nNow, computing $A-(-3)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-3)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-(-3)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(A-(-3)I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -8 & 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get  \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $6a-3b=6\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}-3\\cdot 2=-\\frac{3}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244283 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49045976-e1a2-51c2-9ca2-88751c345e49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241842} {:answer/id #uuid "836e18be-ac0c-5315-8e20-f291161385d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242330} {:answer/id #uuid "ace06c4e-51a4-5dcc-be58-f717c3190652" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242691} {:answer/id #uuid "ae29d905-131b-519f-a343-d64c47f12046" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242699} {:answer/id #uuid "dfa85b8a-8b03-5804-bec0-c6c4da1aece2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242691} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a494214-ab58-55e6-8676-c9fd2150bcd1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243649}] :question/id #uuid "680b02a2-9511-5d6c-a8ce-d85c9645526b" :question/math-academy-id "q-109380" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,J=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & a \\\\ 1 & b\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $a-b.$" :question/worked-solution "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that \n\n- the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2,$\n\n- the second column of $P$ corresponds to the vector ${v}_{2}$ such that $(A-2I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}.$\n\nNow, computing $A-2I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-(2)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(A-2I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=1+{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1+{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get  \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $a-b=2-1=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244284 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "122acd1f-e189-5f8b-94f6-8cfdc98f524f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241334} {:answer/id #uuid "835fec2c-4c36-5a35-85d4-62c834fae2f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242328} {:answer/id #uuid "a0bd4446-4e3f-55dd-9142-11ffa5475ee5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242586} {:answer/id #uuid "db862c9a-3279-5a84-9c19-984858d69bd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243131} {:answer/id #uuid "fb6c3ef9-c6e4-580f-9c70-9529919d3e8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243398}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242328} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b7d7e6a-5058-5a20-b8a3-909ad337a588" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243489}] :question/id #uuid "486b5cfa-a294-5b33-8c93-2adf0abf2ad0" :question/math-academy-id "q-109383" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,J=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & a \\\\ 1 & b\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $a+2b.$" :question/worked-solution "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=7.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that \n\n- the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3,$\n\n- the second column of $P$ corresponds to the vector ${v}_{2}$ such that $(A-7I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}.$\n\nNow, computing $A-7I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-7I & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}-(7)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(A-7I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-1-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get  \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $a+2b=(-3)+2\\cdot 1=-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244285 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "271fc2d1-7d5c-5294-9996-a104d588fb48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241517} {:answer/id #uuid "95e3f816-36d5-5db2-a40a-546832955afe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242495} {:answer/id #uuid "a55b8e37-dd99-5230-a7dc-1dff466eb369" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242636} {:answer/id #uuid "b3c0cbf0-92c2-5920-8c16-ed045791db90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242749} {:answer/id #uuid "ffec570d-e8bc-5495-a263-09672d89cb92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243435}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242749} :answer-field/id #uuid "572f273d-998e-54de-bd7e-19a8e56fc2be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243614}] :question/id #uuid "6a003f2a-e9c1-5590-a615-273be2bf8bf2" :question/math-academy-id "q-109384" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,J=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & a \\\\ 1 & b\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $a-2b.$" :question/worked-solution "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=7.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that \n\n- the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3,$\n\n- the second column of $P$ corresponds to the vector ${v}_{2}$ such that $(A-7I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}.$\n\nNow, computing $A-7I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-7I & =\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-(7)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(A-7I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -8 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -8 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}+2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get  \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $a-2b=\\frac{5}{2}-2\\cdot 1=\\frac{1}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380552 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "273a7235-853a-5a5c-a401-ae2b0efa203f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380554} {:answer/id #uuid "9582a95a-4236-5b4f-aa4a-e5b6ae64e9da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380555} {:answer/id #uuid "fc581734-8939-51b1-8e7f-ff87760e2f0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380556} {:answer/id #uuid "fa522bbf-6e9c-5df4-bc24-317057b8bacb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380557} {:answer/id #uuid "be8a52db-3bc9-53c9-818e-7ea075dc774f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380558}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380558} :answer-field/id #uuid "3361f0b5-e38d-57f0-a3df-c0210c05a1e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380553}] :question/id #uuid "592a790a-f929-5587-9919-fa44768a120c" :question/math-academy-id "q-109376" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,J=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & a \\\\ 1 & b\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $a+b.$" :question/worked-solution "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that \n\n- the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3,$\n\n- the second column of $P$ corresponds to the vector ${v}_{2}$ such that $(A-3I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}.$\n\nNow, computing $A-3I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-(3)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(A-3I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-1-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get  \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $a+b=(-2)+1=-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386425 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eeab0713-efb3-5a44-810a-ce71fc4a5e27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386427} {:answer/id #uuid "d41c3d9d-3db9-50de-aa8d-d24789c4a2fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386428} {:answer/id #uuid "e1b4c4e9-f76d-53e8-9c6c-326b75c5041b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386429} {:answer/id #uuid "d55142bd-e274-5958-ad3c-427da37ad804" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386430} {:answer/id #uuid "301a322f-d080-57b8-a91e-b6de49c6bfc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386431}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386429} :answer-field/id #uuid "d67cfca8-f415-5451-865f-68e0a805ac21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386426}] :question/id #uuid "0993524c-42c2-59d1-8bdb-8f618d952d45" :question/math-academy-id "q-109389" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -12 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{bmatrix},\\,J=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & a \\\\ 1 & b\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $3a+6b.$" :question/worked-solution "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that \n\n- the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3,$\n\n- the second column of $P$ corresponds to the vector ${v}_{2}$ such that $(A-3I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}.$\n\nNow, computing $A-3I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -12 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}-(3)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -12 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(A-3I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -12 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -12 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get  \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $3a+6b=3\\cdot (-\\frac{5}{3})+6\\cdot 1=1.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Elements of a Jordan Canonical Decomposition for a 2x2 Matrix: The Second Column Case"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176010 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175863 :question/id #uuid "b4a6d165-9c52-59e6-af4b-c5777066e877" :question/math-academy-id "e-9227" :question/problem "![](images/54/54ae1bc6614a1fe70fac039fbdd3defd2aeeceb3648679ee3d4c9ad10f6f15b5.png)\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A = P J P^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$\n\n![](images/a9/a985e5861e7360654b98ca334fac6d55f435e271dcc52b313e350f74a4c755c5.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ consists of one Jordan block $J = J_2(1).$ \n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(1) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A-1I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-1I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the second column of $P$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to $\\lambda=1.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-1I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-1I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}-1\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAmong the given vectors, only $\\mathbf x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ since\n\n![](images/62/624e955f479c8193ff32f3bebf0decb7b1d6b45fb5ec44db637d956e3a8d1ea7.png)\nand\n![](images/08/0855b40d83950bcdfeda8447e74187f498fbfe73050e1e8fd7755a1ad4b1dbd0.png)\n\nTherefore, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\overset{A-1I\\,}{\\to }\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\overset{A-1I\\,}{\\to }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe other vectors do not satisfy this condition."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "53e40a15-120c-5587-979b-cc1369d244bf" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176279 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8ccfccb-e7f2-585e-b9cc-f91ac3c75a93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173933} {:answer/id #uuid "321dd3ea-ba51-5475-94ba-8b339cb568cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173934} {:answer/id #uuid "7a801663-6ec6-57ef-995b-7213ccb41503" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173935} {:answer/id #uuid "7f129def-4f64-5326-b8db-c106229c52c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y$ and $z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173936} {:answer/id #uuid "7f56d7d7-a6a3-528d-a539-818bb5161384" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ and $y$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173937}] :answer-field/id #uuid "df5564ab-5e7f-5ba2-8176-df45df2464e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173932}] :question/id #uuid "a817b887-b661-5662-822a-8431c0b997d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-112431" :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, J = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A = PJP^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A$, which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P$?\n\n$x = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}, y = \\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}, z = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176280 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "862dd746-e765-5276-9c3d-a3df22502c34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173939} {:answer/id #uuid "96dc658c-f069-51f0-ab2d-d1edef81492e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173940} {:answer/id #uuid "a76729e7-40ac-5002-883b-ca750e2e549d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y$ and $z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173941} {:answer/id #uuid "d95e6af2-6a09-532e-b47a-027634bf7fca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ and $z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173942} {:answer/id #uuid "5d4fcc32-97fa-52dd-a9a0-5800b1d12609" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173943}] :answer-field/id #uuid "31b89bfc-b72f-5819-96e0-040551b0f540" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173938} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6197fd27-ebb4-589c-a332-928ce1beea75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399734} {:answer/id #uuid "c382c06e-6be3-50d3-afd6-cfb547faf5c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399735} {:answer/id #uuid "9ab49bdc-91a7-5f81-ae26-3dd0344e8f20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ and $z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399736} {:answer/id #uuid "c6333bf7-6d20-5d03-afb9-8cf92d484a71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399737} {:answer/id #uuid "ab6faf66-9395-59a4-b653-5f44c8a58eea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y$ and $z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399738}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399737} :answer-field/id #uuid "af77e6f6-d746-55ef-8323-11da194a2c0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399733}] :question/id #uuid "7e0868ec-2423-5a95-9212-2ae581f51f78" :question/math-academy-id "q-112432" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,J=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,y=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,z=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ consists of one Jordan block $J={J}_{2}(0).$ \n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(0) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A-0I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-0I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ is an eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-0I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-0\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAmong the given vectors, only $x$ is an eigenvector of $A,$ since\n\n$$\n(A-0I)x=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\overset{A-0I\\,}{\\to }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe other vectors do not satisfy this condition."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247276 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47bb13c9-ea10-5be7-8bd3-f4399300487f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$z$ and $x$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244952} {:answer/id #uuid "80ec2645-49cd-5a4e-9570-eafa32c6c531" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245452} {:answer/id #uuid "b26d783a-f272-5ac5-9e90-95bcf1fa38cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ and $y$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245901} {:answer/id #uuid "c087ca23-2090-539a-b0a0-4f2d2fc94d6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246030} {:answer/id #uuid "c412fb11-10c9-5391-b5ec-fb6b8c572a32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246057}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246030} :answer-field/id #uuid "2dead93f-9131-5cb8-933d-92effe391fa2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246621}] :question/id #uuid "51d816dc-8a2d-5a89-8a19-3f1f781666ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-112483" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,J=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,y=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,z=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ consists of one Jordan block $J={J}_{2}(2).$ \n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(2) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ is an eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda =2.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-2I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAmong the given vectors, only $z$ is an eigenvector of $A,$ since\n\n$$\n(A-2I)z=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe other vectors do not satisfy this condition."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247279 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d1dd3e9-ce1c-55f4-9ac3-54d9c87ae4e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244432} {:answer/id #uuid "2f133f55-960d-502c-9cbf-a0934b112a9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244736} {:answer/id #uuid "5734d1a3-b7de-5545-be22-97d6e58bfd49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245082} {:answer/id #uuid "6ee6bdf9-200a-50d2-9247-096288b864f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y$ and $z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245297} {:answer/id #uuid "d3ccb22e-e685-5ed0-a577-a827fc3cca20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ and $z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246200}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244736} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9b36ea4-fa35-5725-8979-1e683f171738" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246963}] :question/id #uuid "91540065-9282-55b2-abc9-b1425d14ef15" :question/math-academy-id "q-112497" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,J=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,y=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,z=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ consists of one Jordan block $J={J}_{2}(2).$ \n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(2) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the second column of $P$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to $\\lambda =2.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-2I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAmong the given vectors, only $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ since\n\n$$\n(A-2I)y=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}=z,\n$$\n\nand, therefore,\n\n$$\n(A-2I)^{2}y=(A-2I)z=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe other vectors do not satisfy this condition."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247280 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cd6359e-e879-56dc-80be-c744f729749a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244568} {:answer/id #uuid "3d028a50-bae5-5910-96a4-509413f16861" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ and $y$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244849} {:answer/id #uuid "619aa510-94ca-53e5-b23e-92547c871c4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$z$ and $x$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245166} {:answer/id #uuid "b32844f5-8b26-56b2-ba36-7f94c1039ffa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245908} {:answer/id #uuid "b5d78b6e-3cb5-5e97-a545-aafeb452e443" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245939}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244568} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9a4ae8f-8502-597a-bc22-2517e2d138f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246991}] :question/id #uuid "7cebb59d-20db-57ac-95ff-8484c20edb72" :question/math-academy-id "q-112505" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & -25 \\\\ 9 & 14\\end{bmatrix},\\,J=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,y=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,z=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ consists of one Jordan block $J={J}_{2}(-1).$ \n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(-1) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A-(-1)I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-(-1)I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ is an eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda =-1.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & -25 \\\\ 9 & 14\\end{bmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-15 & -25 \\\\ 9 & 15\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAmong the given vectors, only $z$ is an eigenvector of $A,$ since\n\n$$\n(A-(-1)I)z=\\begin{bmatrix}-15 & -25 \\\\ 9 & 15\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\overset{A-(-1)I\\,}{\\to }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe other vectors do not satisfy this condition."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247281 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ab273b6-ac77-5088-8e0b-e8c353913328" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$z$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244551} {:answer/id #uuid "5aaf5b77-50ed-51bd-a017-617ba9184766" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ and  $z$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245107} {:answer/id #uuid "71d7539b-2cdb-58d8-9c09-4dd1a7efbcae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245327} {:answer/id #uuid "860d85e9-0be9-5e7a-9eec-84fecf6dde1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245496} {:answer/id #uuid "978d89e9-242b-5882-b245-eb4caebd5d03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y$ and  $z$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245649}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245496} :answer-field/id #uuid "69b34852-250f-5416-9969-746d21e83793" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246731}] :question/id #uuid "00d0e6df-22e0-54bb-9650-150f7f0d8e32" :question/math-academy-id "q-112506" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,J=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,y=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,z=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ consists of one Jordan block $J={J}_{2}(1).$ \n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(1) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A-1I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-1I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ is an eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda =1.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-1I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-1I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}-1\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAmong the given vectors, only $y$ is an eigenvector of $A,$ since\n\n$$\n(A-1I)y=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\overset{A-1I\\,}{\\to }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe other vectors do not satisfy this condition."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380629 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41bea20d-c20b-5a8e-90bc-317d76445d96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380631} {:answer/id #uuid "f5fb3835-f205-50c6-8534-6eee4b2d8bba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380632} {:answer/id #uuid "4223d1fa-e03f-53e8-b601-347c81dae70e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y$ and  $z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380633} {:answer/id #uuid "e05cb1e4-20ff-5ced-b77b-8a7560e167de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$z$ and  $x$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380634} {:answer/id #uuid "8f96e995-682e-5eb8-a2ae-c536de630541" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380635}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380632} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9409570-57d3-5e2c-9365-c691fbd3294a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380630}] :question/id #uuid "87ae16ee-db15-5f83-b8a6-695e01237b18" :question/math-academy-id "q-112494" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,J=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,y=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,z=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ consists of one Jordan block $J={J}_{2}(-1).$ \n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(-1) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A-(-1)I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-(-1)I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the second column of $P$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-1.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAmong the given vectors, only $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ since\n\n$$\n(A-(-1)I)y=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=x,\n$$\n\nand, therefore,\n\n$$\n(A-(-1)I)^{2}y=(A-(-1)I)x=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\overset{A-(-1)I\\,}{\\to }\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\overset{A-(-1)I\\,}{\\to }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe other vectors do not satisfy this condition."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399753 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4405950-ec79-55ae-bb59-7e9cf1458d1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399755} {:answer/id #uuid "94d2993f-9616-511b-a59a-68769f0f70be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399756} {:answer/id #uuid "0b4529ef-30af-5714-a478-69c3cf9b6357" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ and $z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399757} {:answer/id #uuid "102384b8-4bb4-5240-aab6-c8e2bfcdf99c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ and $y$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399758} {:answer/id #uuid "419d4d42-f167-5d71-b60f-b6aab67523fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399759}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399759} :answer-field/id #uuid "12ed4133-1a4b-5aa3-a8c2-be10955c2a4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399754}] :question/id #uuid "f6742def-6999-5043-9553-f817f1ed7b14" :question/math-academy-id "q-112500" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,J=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,y=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,z=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ consists of one Jordan block $J={J}_{2}(-2).$  \n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(-2) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A-(-2)I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-(-2)I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the second column of $P$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-2.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-(-2)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAmong the given vectors, only $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ since\n\n$$\n(A-(-2)I)x=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}=y,\n$$\n\nand, therefore,\n\n$$\n(A-(-2)I)^{2}x=(A+2I)y=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\overset{A-(-2)I\\,}{\\to }\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\overset{A-(-2)I\\,}{\\to }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe other vectors do not satisfy this condition."}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Columns of a Transformation Matrix in a Jordan Decomposition"}] :topic/math-academy-id 3652 :topic/title "Jordan Canonical Decomposition of a 2x2 Matrix"}]}
